"""Original teaching packs for Security group g7.

BODIES maps each topic name to body, r0, r1, r2, r4, and exam.
"""

BODIES = {
    'WPA3': {
        'body': 'WPA3 uses simultaneous authentication of equals so a captured personal handshake is a poor prize for offline word-list grinding. WPA3 personal mode retires the older four-way exchange that handed an observer material worth testing in private later. WPA3 still depends on a passphrase for personal networks, yet that secret is bound into a fresh password-authenticated exchange each time a station associates. WPA3 gives each association its own session keys, so learning one session does not unlock traffic recorded on an earlier visit.\n\nWPA3 makes protected management frames mandatory, which blunts forged deauthentication and disassociation bursts from a casual spoofer. WPA3 enterprise mode can offer a 192-bit suite where sign-in is already certificate-backed and the site is ready for the heavier cipher suite. WPA3 transition mode keeps an older personal mode alive on the same network name, and that compromise reopens the weaker path on purpose. WPA3 belongs on the staff network as the only personal mode once clients can speak it, with legacy stations moved to a separate fenced segment.\n\nWPA3, together with opportunistic wireless encryption, is what six-gigahertz service expects, because older personal modes are not offered there. WPA3 shows up in a beacon robust-security element as an SAE capability and as management-frame protection that is required rather than optional. WPA3 does not by itself issue each person a distinct account, because per-person sign-in is an authentication-server decision sitting beside the air protection. WPA3 does not choose which programs a handset may install, because an allow-list lives on the device policy, not in the radio handshake.\n\nWPA3 code on controllers and access points has had its own defects, so a supported mode is unfinished until firmware follows the vendor fix stream. WPA3 configuration should drop older-only operation on the staff name after every maintained client can complete the new exchange. WPA3 guest service should use a different name and a different isolation rule, never a staff secret posted where visitors can read it. WPA3 passphrase quality still matters against online guessing at the access point, which is slower and noisier than a private grind of captured material.\n\nWPA3 controller logs should show which stations negotiated the new mode and which stations attempted a fallback. WPA3 survey notes should say whether management-frame protection is required on that name or merely advertised as capable. WPA3 client failures after a driver update are compatibility work, not a reason to put the staff name back into a mixed mode. WPA3 personal cipher choice is normally CCMP with AES, while the enterprise 192-bit profile steps the suite upward.\n\nWPA3 forward secrecy means frames captured last month stay unreadable even if the passphrase is learned this month. WPA3 on one staff name should not advertise both generations once the client fleet can be moved, because the older advertisement is a finding. WPA3 onboarding notes should tell people to forget a cached older profile so the station does not prefer it in the background. WPA3 enterprise failures are usually certificate or server problems, whereas a personal-mode failure is a passphrase problem or an SAE negotiation problem.\n\nWPA3 does not replace rogue-access-point detection, because a lookalike name can still be advertised by equipment you do not own. WPA3 is not client isolation, band steering, or transmit-power choice, and a ticket should not use those labels when the evidence is the handshake family. WPA3 tickets that say only that wireless feels weak are incomplete until the mode, the transition flag, and the network name are written down. WPA3 changes belong on a spare name first, so a bad controller image can be reversed before voice handsets are stranded.\n\nWPA3 designs leave the older temporal-key integrity protocol behind, and that protocol should not remain enabled beside staff traffic. WPA3 personal secrets shared by a whole shift are still an identity weakness even when the air protection itself is current. WPA3 reviews should list every name that still allows the older personal mode and which segment those stations can reach. WPA3 evidence can be a beacon captured without joining, which is enough to read the advertised protection suite.\n\nWPA3 station tables on the controller should show the pairwise cipher and whether management frames on that association are protected. WPA3 does nothing to remove a departed person when the secret is a shared passphrase, which is why staff wireless should not stop at personal mode. WPA3 standards rows should name the controller, the site, the network name, and the modes that row allows. WPA3 legacy printers can sit on a segment that cannot open sessions toward staff hosts, rather than pulling the staff name backward.',
        'r0': "WPA3 personal links run a password-authenticated key agreement called SAE instead of recycling one pre-shared master secret an observer can grind through from a capture. WPA3 derives fresh session keys per association, which is forward secrecy for frames on the air. WPA3 forces protected management frames, so spoofed deauthentication is no longer a cheap way to knock stations off. WPA3 enterprise mode may step up to a 192-bit profile when certificates and stronger ciphers are already the site standard. WPA3 transition mode is only a bridge, because the older personal handshake stays available beside the new one. WPA3 on six-gigahertz radios is the baseline the band actually allows. WPA3 proof is the beacon suite plus the station's negotiated cipher, not a guess that the name sounds current.",
        'r1': 'WPA3 is newer wifi protection. WPA3 hinders offline passphrase grinding. WPA3 still has a personal passphrase. WPA3 makes fresh keys per join. WPA3 requires protected management frames. WPA3 transition mode keeps an older path. WPA3 staff names should drop mixed mode. WPA3 legacy clients use a fenced segment. WPA3 is not directory login per person. WPA3 is not a handset app allow-list. WPA3 gear still needs current firmware. WPA3 beacons show the real suite. WPA3 proof is the negotiated suite.',
        'r2': "WPA3 resembles a concert wristband whose hologram is pressed again for every performance. WPA3 in that picture makes a photo of last night's band useless at tonight's gate. WPA3 transition mode is the manager who still honors last year's paper strip. WPA3 for the staff entrance should refuse the paper strip. WPA3 legacy visitors can walk a side garden that never opens onto the stage. WPA3 protected management frames are ushers who ignore a stranger yelling that someone was removed. WPA3 does not decide which instruments may come through the loading dock. WPA3 firmware is the press, and a damaged press prints weak holograms until the shop repairs it. WPA3 evidence is the hologram a door scanner actually reads, not a poster claiming the venue is modern.",
        'r4': 'WPA3: record the staff mode before any change. WPA3: require protected management frames on that name. WPA3: disable transition mode after clients are ready. WPA3: move older-only devices onto a fenced segment. WPA3: capture a beacon and read the suite. WPA3: treat per-person login as a separate choice. WPA3: patch controller firmware before closing the task. WPA3: write a backout if roaming handsets fail.',
        'exam': 'WPA3 items turn on a personal handshake that resists offline grinding and on management frames that must be protected. WPA3 fits when a beacon shows SAE and the staff name is not left mixed for convenience. WPA3 is the distractor when the stem describes one directory account per person. WPA3 is also the distractor when the stem is a ban on side-loaded handset programs.',
    },
    'RADIUS for wireless': {
        'body': "RADIUS for wireless lets the access point act as an authenticator while a central server decides whether a person's credentials are good. RADIUS for wireless sits inside 802.1X, with the station as the supplicant and an EAP method carrying the proof. RADIUS for wireless ties each association to a directory account, so disabling that account removes the ability to join at the next authentication. RADIUS for wireless replaces a passphrase known to a whole shift with a credential that can be revoked for one person.\n\nRADIUS for wireless accounting records who associated, from which called-station identifier, and when the session started and stopped. RADIUS for wireless uses a shared device secret between the network access server and the authentication server, and that secret is not the person's password. RADIUS for wireless commonly listens on UDP 1812 for access requests and UDP 1813 for accounting, with older deployments sometimes still on 1645 and 1646. RADIUS for wireless can return a VLAN, a filter name, or a session timeout inside the access-accept so the controller places the station in the right segment.\n\nRADIUS for wireless prefers EAP-TLS when both the station and the server present certificates, because a stolen password database does not by itself complete that method. RADIUS for wireless may use PEAP or EAP-TTLS when a password is tunneled inside TLS, and the outer tunnel must validate the server certificate. RADIUS for wireless failure after a certificate rollover is often a trust-anchor problem on the phone, not a radio-power problem. RADIUS for wireless does not choose the pairwise cipher by itself; the robust-security negotiation still has to land on a current suite such as WPA3-Enterprise.\n\nRADIUS for wireless is the wrong label for a personal SAE passphrase, even when that passphrase is long. RADIUS for wireless is the wrong label for an allow-list of phone applications, which never consults the authentication server. RADIUS for wireless change of authorization, often on UDP 3799, lets the server disconnect or reauthorize a session when group membership changes. RADIUS for wireless shared secrets should differ per controller and should not be the vendor string left in the sample config.\n\nRADIUS for wireless logs on the server should be retained off the access point so a stolen radio does not take the only copy of who joined. RADIUS for wireless test accounts belong to a lab policy and should not be a standing backdoor on the staff SSID. RADIUS for wireless dynamic VLAN assignment needs a matching trunk and a DHCP scope, or the station authenticates and then gets nowhere. RADIUS for wireless machine authentication and user authentication answer different questions, and a policy should say which one grants staff reach.\n\nRADIUS for wireless certificate templates for EAP-TLS should name the purpose, the lifetime, and who may enroll a device. RADIUS for wireless inner methods that still depend on a reversible or weakly hashed password are a reason to move those users toward certificates. RADIUS for wireless guest portals that only collect an email address are not this control, because no directory account was proven. RADIUS for wireless redundancy means at least two servers and a tested failover, since a single authentication host becomes a door stop for every radio.\n\nRADIUS for wireless timing should account for elevator lobbies and voice roam, where a slow EAP exchange drops calls even though the password was correct. RADIUS for wireless attributes must be documented so a firewall change does not strip the VLAN reply and dump staff into a dead segment. RADIUS for wireless identity strings should match the directory, and a typo in the outer identity can hide the real account from the log. RADIUS for wireless MAC filters are a weak neighbor, not a substitute, because MAC addresses are trivial to observe and replay.\n\nRADIUS for wireless onboarding should retire the old shared passphrase SSID the same week the directory method is turned up. RADIUS for wireless evidence on a ticket is an access-accept or access-reject with a user name, a NAS identifier, and a timestamp. RADIUS for wireless rejects with a quiet timeout point at reachability or the shared secret, while an access-reject points at the credential or the policy. RADIUS for wireless interim accounting helps when sessions last all day and a disconnect packet is lost.\n\nRADIUS for wireless privilege should not ride in a group that also administers the directory, or wireless support staff inherit unrelated rights. RADIUS for wireless client supplicants need a profile that pins the server name, or users will tap through a warning and train themselves to ignore it.",
        'r0': 'RADIUS for wireless places 802.1X between the station, the access point, and an authentication server that speaks the RADIUS protocol. RADIUS for wireless proves a directory identity with EAP, then returns authorization such as a VLAN in the access-accept. RADIUS for wireless accounting on UDP 1813 records the session beside the authentication on UDP 1812. RADIUS for wireless device secret authenticates the radio to the server and is distinct from the human credential. RADIUS for wireless with EAP-TLS uses certificates, while PEAP and EAP-TTLS can tunnel a password inside a validated TLS outer layer. RADIUS for wireless can send disconnect or change-of-authorization messages when the directory group changes. RADIUS for wireless evidence is the accept or reject line, not the brand of the access point.',
        'r1': 'RADIUS for wireless gives each person an account. RADIUS for wireless drops access when that account is disabled. RADIUS for wireless is not a shared passphrase. RADIUS for wireless uses 802.1X and EAP. RADIUS for wireless logs who joined and when. RADIUS for wireless can place a station in a VLAN. RADIUS for wireless likes certificates via EAP-TLS. RADIUS for wireless still needs a current cipher suite. RADIUS for wireless is not an app allow-list. RADIUS for wireless needs two servers for failover. RADIUS for wireless proof is the accept or reject.',
        'r2': 'RADIUS for wireless is the library desk that looks up your own card before it lets you into the stacks. RADIUS for wireless cancels that card the afternoon the patron leaves, and the turnstile refuses the next visit. RADIUS for wireless is not a chalkboard code shouted to every reader in the lobby. RADIUS for wireless writes a line in the checkout ledger naming the person and the hour. RADIUS for wireless can send the reader to the local-history room or the map room based on the card type. RADIUS for wireless still needs a sturdy door on the building, because a valid card does not invent a lock. RADIUS for wireless fails closed when the catalog computer is down unless a second catalog computer is ready. RADIUS for wireless proof is the ledger line, not a rumor that the building feels exclusive.',
        'r4': 'RADIUS for wireless: bind the staff name to directory accounts. RADIUS for wireless: disable a test account and confirm the join fails. RADIUS for wireless: require server-certificate checks in the supplicant. RADIUS for wireless: set a unique device secret per controller. RADIUS for wireless: log accepts and rejects off the access point. RADIUS for wireless: map each group to a VLAN and test it. RADIUS for wireless: document EAP method, servers, and failover. RADIUS for wireless: retire the shared passphrase name the same week.',
        'exam': 'RADIUS for wireless items describe 802.1X with a per-person directory proof and an access-accept. RADIUS for wireless is correct when disabling the account stops the next association. RADIUS for wireless is incorrect when the only secret is a passphrase shared by a shift. RADIUS for wireless is incorrect when the stem is about which phone programs may install. RADIUS for wireless still assumes the air suite is negotiated separately from the account check.',
    },
    'Mobile application control': {
        'body': "Mobile application control decides which programs may be installed or run on a managed handset, usually from an allow-list rather than a hope that people choose well. Mobile application control blocks sideloading and unknown sources so a game file from a message cannot land beside work mail. Mobile application control can pin a minimum version, which matters when an old build of a required app has a known defect. Mobile application control may push configuration into a managed app, such as a server name, without asking the person to type it.\n\nMobile application control lives in the device policy or the work profile, and it does not replace the radio protection mode on the building wireless. Mobile application control is not the same decision as detecting a jailbreak, though a rooted phone may also be non-compliant for a different reason. Mobile application control should inventory what is actually installed, because an allow-list that nobody compares to the device is a PDF. Mobile application control deny-lists help against a few named bad packages and fail open against everything unnamed, so high-risk roles deserve an allow-list.\n\nMobile application control for a work profile can remove the work apps without deleting personal photos when the person leaves. Mobile application control should treat developer mode and USB debugging as separate hardening items that often travel with sideloading. Mobile application control evidence is a console event showing an install blocked, plus the package name and the device identifier. Mobile application control exceptions need an owner and an expiry, or a one-time test build becomes a permanent hole.\n\nMobile application control does not prove the allowed app is free of flaws; it only narrows who may introduce a new binary. Mobile application control pairing with an app-reputation feed can flag a newly malicious package that was previously allowed. Mobile application control on unsupervised personal phones is weaker, because the organization may only steer the work container. Mobile application control should block consumer app stores on corporate-owned devices that have no personal use.\n\nMobile application control rollout belongs in a pilot group, since a bad allow-list can knock a clinical app offline at the start of a shift. Mobile application control logs should distinguish a user-cancelled install from a policy deny, or the metric lies. Mobile application control for web clips and progressive web apps needs a rule too, because not every work tool arrives as a store package. Mobile application control permissions inside the app, such as microphone or photo access, are a second layer after the install decision.\n\nMobile application control should stop legacy enterprise apps that ask the user to trust a private certificate without a managed deployment. Mobile application control review each quarter should remove packages nobody uses, shrinking the set you must patch. Mobile application control is the wrong label when the finding is only that storage is unencrypted. Mobile application control is the wrong label when the finding is a shared wireless passphrase.\n\nMobile application control communication to staff should say which store is allowed and what a blocked install looks like. Mobile application control for loaner devices must reset the app set between borrowers so one person's sideload does not survive. Mobile application control can require the managed browser for work links so a random browser with extra plugins is not the path to the intranet. Mobile application control backup of the policy should live with the other device standards, not only inside one admin's console bookmark.\n\nMobile application control testing should include a deliberately unapproved package and record that the handset refused it. Mobile application control does not by itself wipe data after theft; removal of apps may be part of a selective wipe, which is a neighboring action. Mobile application control age of the allow-list is a finding when new hires cannot get the current clinical app because the list froze last year. Mobile application control vendor kiosks that run a single locked app are a strict form of the same idea.\n\nMobile application control should name the platform, because an allow-list for one phone family does not automatically exist on the other. Mobile application control alerts on new package identifiers in the fleet catch shadow installs that a stale report missed. Mobile application control for contractors should expire with the contract, taking their managed apps with the account. Mobile application control documentation lists the allowed package identifiers, the store, the owner, and the exception path.\n\nMobile application control is effective when a test phone outside the list cannot add the game, and a phone inside the list can add the clinical app and nothing else.",
        'r0': 'Mobile application control enforces an install policy, commonly an allow-list, and shuts unknown sources so sideloaded packages never become work tools. Mobile application control can require a minimum app version and can push managed configuration such as a host name. Mobile application control inventory is the check that the policy and the handset still match. Mobile application control deny-lists cover named packages only, so they are a weaker pattern than an allow-list for sensitive roles. Mobile application control events should show the package name, the device, and whether the result was a policy deny. Mobile application control does not set the wireless handshake and does not by itself encrypt the disk. Mobile application control exceptions need an owner and a date or they become forgotten holes.',
        'r1': 'Mobile application control limits which apps may install. Mobile application control blocks sideloading from unknown sources. Mobile application control often uses an allow-list. Mobile application control can require a minimum version. Mobile application control is not the wifi handshake. Mobile application control is not jailbreak detection. Mobile application control is not disk encryption. Mobile application control should log blocked package names. Mobile application control exceptions need an owner and a date. Mobile application control must be tested with a bad app. Mobile application control lives in device policy.',
        'r2': "Mobile application control is the supply closet that issues only the tools listed on the wall chart. Mobile application control turns away a toolbox a stranger handed the driver in the lot. Mobile application control can insist the wrench be the current model, not a cracked one from three seasons ago. Mobile application control writes the borrower's name when something is refused. Mobile application control is not the lock on the truck door and not the alarm that screams if the door was cut. Mobile application control for a short-term driver ends when the route ends, and the tools come back. Mobile application control fails if the chart is never compared with what is actually on the bench. Mobile application control proof is a refused tool with a name, not a vague sense that the lot looks tidy.",
        'r4': 'Mobile application control: publish the allow-list with package names. Mobile application control: block unknown sources on managed devices. Mobile application control: pilot the list before the whole shift. Mobile application control: record a blocked install with the package name. Mobile application control: expire every exception with an owner. Mobile application control: remove unused packages each quarter. Mobile application control: separate this policy from wipe and from wireless. Mobile application control: retest after a store or console change.',
        'exam': 'Mobile application control items ask what may be installed, not how the radio authenticates. Mobile application control is right when sideloading is blocked and an allow-list names the packages. Mobile application control is wrong when the evidence is only SAE on a beacon. Mobile application control is wrong when the evidence is only a rooted phone losing mail. Mobile application control exceptions without a date are the usual sloppy distractor.',
    },
    'Jailbreak and root detection': {
        'body': "Jailbreak and root detection looks for signs that the phone's platform protections were removed, such as a privilege-escalation binary, a modified system partition, or an unsigned code path the vendor does not ship. Jailbreak and root detection then feeds a compliance decision, commonly to withhold company mail and work apps until the device is restored or replaced. Jailbreak and root detection is not proof against a careful bypass, so the control belongs with other checks rather than as a lone promise of a trustworthy handset. Jailbreak and root detection on one family of phones is often called a jailbreak, and on another it is called root, but the policy question is the same: the vendor trust model was broken.\n\nJailbreak and root detection should run at enrollment and again on a schedule, because a phone can be altered the week after it looked healthy. Jailbreak and root detection results belong in the compliance report with a timestamp, not only as a red icon one admin remembers. Jailbreak and root detection is a different finding from a missing screen lock or from unencrypted storage. Jailbreak and root detection is a different action from a remote wipe, though the same console may offer both buttons.\n\nJailbreak and root detection is not sandboxing, which confines a program on a still-intact operating system. Jailbreak and root detection signals can include failed device attestation, the presence of known exploitation packages, or a boot chain that no longer matches the vendor. Jailbreak and root detection should not be documented as a set of steps for hiding those signals. Jailbreak and root detection false alarms happen after some vendor updates, so a sudden fleet-wide failure needs a check against the console vendor before hundreds of people lose mail.\n\nJailbreak and root detection policy for personal phones may quarantine only the work profile, leaving personal photos in place. Jailbreak and root detection on a corporate-owned phone can justify a full block plus a rebuild. Jailbreak and root detection does not repair the phone; someone still has to reinstall a stock image or send the device to support. Jailbreak and root detection evidence for a ticket is the compliance state, the signal that fired, and whether company data was actually blocked.\n\nJailbreak and root detection bypasses traded in forums are a reason to keep the check, not a reason to abandon it, as long as the team treats it as one layer. Jailbreak and root detection should be paired with an app policy so a rooted phone cannot simply sideload a private copy of mail. Jailbreak and root detection during incident work matters because a rooted phone is a poor place to collect trustworthy logs. Jailbreak and root detection exemptions for a lab device must name the device and keep it off production mail.\n\nJailbreak and root detection after a merger needs one standard, or the acquired fleet stays invisible to the new console. Jailbreak and root detection communication should tell staff that removing platform protections drops work access, without teaching them how the removal is done. Jailbreak and root detection metrics worth keeping are the count of non-compliant devices and the hours until data access was stopped. Jailbreak and root detection is the wrong label when the only fact is that a passphrase lock timed out too slowly.\n\nJailbreak and root detection is the wrong label when the only fact is that an attachment opened inside a confined viewer.",
        'r0': 'Jailbreak and root detection flags a handset whose vendor protections were stripped, then the policy can block company profiles. Jailbreak and root detection should repeat after enrollment because the device can change later. Jailbreak and root detection is one layer, since a determined person may hide the signal, and the console can also be wrong after a vendor update. Jailbreak and root detection evidence is a timestamped compliance state plus the action taken on mail or apps. Jailbreak and root detection does not replace storage encryption, a screen lock, a remote wipe, or a sandbox. Jailbreak and root detection lab exceptions must name a device that never holds production data.',
        'r1': 'Jailbreak and root detection spots removed platform protections. Jailbreak and root detection can block company mail afterward. Jailbreak and root detection is not a perfect proof. Jailbreak and root detection should repeat on a schedule. Jailbreak and root detection is not a screen lock. Jailbreak and root detection is not storage encryption. Jailbreak and root detection is not a remote wipe. Jailbreak and root detection is not a sandbox. Jailbreak and root detection needs a timestamped result. Jailbreak and root detection lab phones stay off production.',
        'r2': 'Jailbreak and root detection is the gallery guard who notices the alarm wire on a display case has been cut. Jailbreak and root detection then refuses to hang the traveling exhibit in that case. Jailbreak and root detection does not claim every cut wire will be seen, so the gallery still locks the storeroom. Jailbreak and root detection is not the same as closing the lid, lining the case with felt, or sending the exhibit home. Jailbreak and root detection after a false scare, when a new frame looks odd but is still factory-made, needs a calm second look before the whole museum is emptied. Jailbreak and root detection for a teaching replica can be waived only when that replica never holds the real exhibit. Jailbreak and root detection writes the time the wire was found and what was removed from the case.',
        'r4': 'Jailbreak and root detection: check compliance at enrollment and on a schedule. Jailbreak and root detection: block company profiles when the signal fires. Jailbreak and root detection: record the signal, the time, and the device. Jailbreak and root detection: confirm a fleet-wide spike against the vendor first. Jailbreak and root detection: keep lab devices off production data. Jailbreak and root detection: do not write bypass steps into the runbook. Jailbreak and root detection: pair the check with an app allow-list. Jailbreak and root detection: restore or replace the phone before access returns.',
        'exam': 'Jailbreak and root detection items describe a handset whose platform protections were removed and a policy that then blocks company data. Jailbreak and root detection is not the label for a missing PIN or for ciphertext on storage. Jailbreak and root detection is not the label for an erase command sent from the console. Jailbreak and root detection is not the label for opening a file inside a confined viewer.',
    },
    'Screen locks and full-device encryption': {
        'body': "Screen locks and full-device encryption answer two different thefts, and a lost handset needs both. Screen locks and full-device encryption use the lock to stop a person who picks up an awake or sleeping phone and tries the glass. Screen locks and full-device encryption use ciphertext on storage so a chip pulled from the board is not a readable disk. Screen locks and full-device encryption fail as a pair when the lock is missing, because an unlocked running phone can expose keys that encryption already unwrapped.\n\nScreen locks and full-device encryption also fail as a pair when storage is plain, because the lock password never enters the story once the flash is unsoldered. Screen locks and full-device encryption should set a short idle timeout and a wipe or delay after repeated bad guesses, tuned so clinical shifts are not locked out every hour. Screen locks and full-device encryption can accept a biometric as a convenience that still unlocks a secret stored in hardware, and the policy should say what happens after a reboot when the biometric is not enough. Screen locks and full-device encryption on modern phones is often file-based, with some files available only after the first unlock and some device data protected earlier.\n\nScreen locks and full-device encryption evidence is a compliance readout that says storage is encrypted and that a passphrase, PIN, or equivalent is required. Screen locks and full-device encryption is not the detector that notices a removed platform protection. Screen locks and full-device encryption is not the command that erases a stolen tablet from the console. Screen locks and full-device encryption is not the confined viewer that opens one attachment away from the user's documents.\n\nScreen locks and full-device encryption standards should ban trivial PINs and patterns that a shoulder-surfer can replay. Screen locks and full-device encryption for a work profile may encrypt the work container even when a personal phone's photo roll uses a different setting. Screen locks and full-device encryption after a repair depot visit should be rechecked, because a board swap can return a device that boots without the expected ciphertext. Screen locks and full-device encryption keys belong in a hardware-backed store, not in a file an app can simply copy.\n\nScreen locks and full-device encryption communication should tell people that a lock screen photo of their children is not the control, the secret is. Screen locks and full-device encryption on laptops follows the same split: a logged-on session is a glass-door problem, and a stolen disk is a ciphertext problem. Screen locks and full-device encryption exceptions for kiosks must explain how the kiosk is physically held and what data it cannot store. Screen locks and full-device encryption testing can be a compliance report sample plus one device whose flash cannot be read as files when the secret is unknown.\n\nScreen locks and full-device encryption rollout should precede mail delivery, so the first sync does not land on a plaintext phone. Screen locks and full-device encryption recovery codes, if the platform offers them, must be stored as carefully as the data they protect. Screen locks and full-device encryption is incomplete when encryption is on but the lock timeout is measured in hours in a public clinic. Screen locks and full-device encryption on a shared clinic tablet should use a distinct secret per shift worker only when the platform supports fast user switching; otherwise the device stays single-user.\n\nScreen locks and full-device encryption status should be rechecked after an operating-system upgrade because a migration can boot once without the expected lock policy.",
        'r0': 'Screen locks and full-device encryption pair a gate on the glass with ciphertext on the flash. Screen locks and full-device encryption stop a casual finder with the lock and stop a chip-off read with the encryption. Screen locks and full-device encryption still needs a real secret, because a biometric after boot may depend on that secret having been entered. Screen locks and full-device encryption compliance should show both a required lock and an encrypted storage state. Screen locks and full-device encryption is a different control from jailbreak detection, remote erase, and document confinement. Screen locks and full-device encryption for laptops follows the same split between a live session and a removed disk.',
        'r1': 'Screen locks and full-device encryption stop two thefts. Screen locks and full-device encryption use a lock on the glass. Screen locks and full-device encryption use ciphertext on storage. Screen locks and full-device encryption need both parts present. Screen locks and full-device encryption is not jailbreak detection. Screen locks and full-device encryption is not a remote erase. Screen locks and full-device encryption is not a document sandbox. Screen locks and full-device encryption should use a short timeout. Screen locks and full-device encryption evidence is a compliance readout. Screen locks and full-device encryption comes before mail sync.',
        'r2': 'Screen locks and full-device encryption is a diary clasp plus a strongbox around the pages. Screen locks and full-device encryption keeps a stranger from reading the open diary on the bench. Screen locks and full-device encryption keeps a stranger who steals only the paper from reading it later. Screen locks and full-device encryption fails if the clasp is never shut, even when the pages were pressed in wax. Screen locks and full-device encryption fails if the pages are ordinary paper, even when the clasp looks serious. Screen locks and full-device encryption is not a sensor that notices a broken hinge, not a courier sent to burn the diary, and not a reading room that shows one page through a slit. Screen locks and full-device encryption proof is a clasp that actually catches and pages that are not readable as plain paper.',
        'r4': 'Screen locks and full-device encryption: require a secret and a short idle timeout. Screen locks and full-device encryption: confirm storage reports ciphertext before mail is allowed. Screen locks and full-device encryption: ban trivial PINs and obvious patterns. Screen locks and full-device encryption: recheck devices after depot repair. Screen locks and full-device encryption: keep recovery codes as sensitive as the phone. Screen locks and full-device encryption: do not call this a wipe or a jailbreak finding. Screen locks and full-device encryption: sample the compliance report each month. Screen locks and full-device encryption: document any kiosk that cannot carry a normal lock.',
        'exam': 'Screen locks and full-device encryption items want both a gate on the glass and ciphertext on storage. Screen locks and full-device encryption is the wrong choice when the stem only says the platform protections were removed. Screen locks and full-device encryption is the wrong choice when the stem is an erase sent from a console. Screen locks and full-device encryption is the wrong choice when the stem is an attachment opened in a confined viewer.',
    },
    'Remote wipe': {
        'body': "Remote wipe sends an erase from a management console to a device that is lost, stolen, or held by someone who has left. Remote wipe may clear only the work profile, or it may erase the whole device when the organization owns the hardware. Remote wipe usually waits for the next check-in, so a phone that stays offline in a drawer is not erased until it sees the network again. Remote wipe on an encrypted device can be a crypto-erase that destroys the key rather than overwriting every block.\n\nRemote wipe is a poor substitute for encryption if the thief never lets the device check in. Remote wipe should be authorized by the lost-device procedure, not by whoever happens to have the console open. Remote wipe confirmation belongs in the audit log with the requester, the device identifier, and the time the device acknowledged. Remote wipe of a work profile should leave personal photos when the policy promised that, and the test plan should prove it.\n\nRemote wipe of a corporate-owned phone can be a full erase plus a disable of the account that would simply re-sync mail. Remote wipe is not the detector for a jailbreak, though both may appear in the same console. Remote wipe is not the screen lock and is not storage encryption, which protect the device before anyone sends a command. Remote wipe is not sandboxing of one document.\n\nRemote wipe for a laptop may mean a management agent erase or a directory disable plus a bitlocker-style key destruction, and the runbook should name which one this fleet actually has. Remote wipe orders that arrive after the device was already recovered should be cancelled so a returned phone is not erased in the owner's hands by mistake. Remote wipe testing belongs on a spare device, including the case where the device is offline at the moment of the order and checks in an hour later. Remote wipe communication to staff should say whom to call and what will be erased.\n\nRemote wipe of dual-SIM or dual-profile phones needs a clear scope so the personal line is not destroyed by habit. Remote wipe evidence on a ticket is the command, the acknowledgement, and what data the policy said would disappear. Remote wipe failures should escalate to account disable and, if the disk was encrypted, to comfort that offline reads are still hard. Remote wipe retention of the console log matters when someone later asks whether the erase really happened.\n\nRemote wipe rights in the console should be few, because the button is destructive. Remote wipe after a termination should run the same day, together with badge and account removal, not at the next quarterly cleanup. Remote wipe does not recover the device; it only reduces what a finder can read if the command lands. Remote wipe scope should be written in the lost-device play before the night shift needs it, including whether messaging apps outside the work profile are in bounds.\n\nRemote wipe of a phone that also holds authenticator seeds should be paired with revoking those seeds in the identity service so a wiped device is not the only place the codes die. Remote wipe retries belong on a timer when the first acknowledgement never arrives, and the ticket should show each attempt. Remote wipe of a laptop in a docking station can succeed on the corporate interface while a personal hotspot keeps a session alive, so the runbook names every path.",
        'r0': 'Remote wipe is a console command that erases a work profile or an entire device after loss or departure. Remote wipe depends on a later check-in unless the device is already online. Remote wipe can destroy encryption keys instead of rewriting every sector when the platform supports crypto-erase. Remote wipe without encryption leaves a gap for any thief who keeps the radio off. Remote wipe audit lines should name the requester, the device, and the acknowledgement time. Remote wipe is distinct from jailbreak detection, from the lock-and-encryption pair, and from confining one document. Remote wipe testing uses a spare device that is offline when the order is sent. Remote wipe orders should name the profile scope before the button is pressed.',
        'r1': 'Remote wipe erases a lost or returned device from afar. Remote wipe may clear only work data. Remote wipe may erase a company-owned phone entirely. Remote wipe waits until the device checks in. Remote wipe can destroy encryption keys. Remote wipe is weak if the phone stays offline. Remote wipe is not jailbreak detection. Remote wipe is not the screen lock. Remote wipe is not a document sandbox. Remote wipe needs an audit line. Remote wipe should be tested on a spare. Remote wipe rights belong to few people.',
        'r2': "Remote wipe is a greenhouse valve closed from the office when a cold frame goes missing. Remote wipe can shut only the research mist line, or it can drain the whole company house if the company owns every bench. Remote wipe does nothing while the frame is unplugged in a stranger's shed, until someone plugs it back in. Remote wipe of the water key is faster than scrubbing every leaf by hand when the beds were already locked under wax. Remote wipe is not the sensor that noticed a cut latch, not the clasp on the door, and not a single plant kept in its own pot. Remote wipe writes who turned the valve and when the frame answered. Remote wipe should be practiced on a spare frame so the first real loss is not the first time the valve sticks.",
        'r4': 'Remote wipe: follow the lost-device procedure before sending the command. Remote wipe: choose work-profile or full erase on purpose. Remote wipe: disable the account so mail cannot simply return. Remote wipe: record requester, device, and acknowledgement. Remote wipe: cancel the order if the device is recovered first. Remote wipe: test an offline spare that checks in later. Remote wipe: limit who holds the console button. Remote wipe: run the same day someone leaves.',
        'exam': 'Remote wipe items are about an erase command for loss or departure, not about noticing a rooted phone. Remote wipe is correct when the console order and the device acknowledgement are the evidence. Remote wipe is incorrect when the stem only describes a PIN plus ciphertext. Remote wipe is incorrect when the stem only describes a confined attachment viewer. Remote wipe that never reaches an offline phone is why encryption still matters beside it.',
    },
    'Sandboxing': {
        'body': "Sandboxing places untrusted code or a risky document inside a narrow grant so a crash or a hostile payload cannot automatically take the whole computer. Sandboxing restricts what that space can read, often blocking the person's document folders, and it restricts what the space can do to the network or to other processes. Sandboxing on a desktop may be a protected view for mail attachments, a browser renderer process with a tight system-call filter, or an application container with its own file tree. Sandboxing is not a claim that the confined program is harmless; it is a claim that the blast radius was narrowed.\n\nSandboxing escapes are themselves defects, and the response is to patch the boundary, not to publish a recipe for crossing it. Sandboxing does not replace patching the application, because a confined bug can still steal what the sandbox is allowed to touch. Sandboxing is not jailbreak detection, which asks whether the phone's own platform walls were removed. Sandboxing is not a screen lock and is not full-device encryption.\n\nSandboxing is not a remote wipe. Sandboxing policy for mail should say which attachment types open in the confined viewer and which types are blocked outright. Sandboxing for browsers should keep untrusted sites in a process that cannot read the password store or the local documents. Sandboxing evidence is the policy setting plus a test that an opened sample could not reach a canary file outside the box.\n\nSandboxing on a build server can confine a dependency install so a malicious package script does not rewrite the runner's home directory. Sandboxing limits should be written down, because an overly wide exception, such as granting the box the entire home directory, quietly deletes the control. Sandboxing logs that record a denied open of a sensitive path are useful during an incident. Sandboxing for student or kiosk machines can reset the confined profile at logoff.\n\nSandboxing is a different layer from an allow-list of mobile apps, though both reduce what untrusted code can do. Sandboxing performance complaints are not a reason to disable it fleet-wide without a named exception. Sandboxing after an escape bug should be treated as an urgent patch, with the confined feature left on. Sandboxing documentation should name the product feature, the allowed paths, and the test canary.\n\nSandboxing a single macro-enabled document is a common office case, and the macro should not see the rest of the share just because the user can. Sandboxing does not decide wireless authentication and does not classify an asset. Sandboxing on a mail gateway can detonate an attachment in a disposable virtual machine and return only a verdict, which is a stronger cage than a local protected view. Sandboxing rules for that detonation should forbid the cage from using the organization's real credentials or from reaching internal applications.\n\nSandboxing of browser tabs can isolate one site per process so a hostile script cannot read another tab's memory. Sandboxing grants for a developer tool should be narrower than the grant for a trusted build, or the tool becomes a tunnel past the cage. Sandboxing telemetry that records a denied system call helps responders see what the sample tried, without needing a full memory image. Sandboxing exceptions requested for a legacy plugin should name the plugin, the path it needs, and a removal date.\n\nSandboxing of office macros can allow signed macros from an internal publisher and block the rest.",
        'r0': 'Sandboxing confines a process or a document so its reads, writes, and system calls stay inside a narrow grant. Sandboxing examples include a mail protected view, a browser renderer with a system-call filter, and a container with a private file tree. Sandboxing shrinks blast radius and still needs the application patched. Sandboxing escapes are defects in the boundary and should be fixed without a public how-to. Sandboxing is not jailbreak detection, not the lock-and-encryption pair, and not a remote erase. Sandboxing proof is a canary file the confined sample could not open. Sandboxing of a detonated attachment should use a disposable machine that holds no production credentials. Sandboxing grants need a written list, because an unnamed allow becomes a hole.',
        'r1': 'Sandboxing confines untrusted code or documents. Sandboxing limits files the program can read. Sandboxing can limit network and system calls. Sandboxing narrows the blast radius. Sandboxing does not replace patching. Sandboxing escapes are boundary defects. Sandboxing is not jailbreak detection. Sandboxing is not a screen lock. Sandboxing is not disk encryption. Sandboxing is not a remote wipe. Sandboxing proof uses a canary file. Sandboxing exceptions must stay narrow. Sandboxing can detonate files in a disposable machine. Sandboxing should withhold production credentials from that cage.',
        'r2': 'Sandboxing is a spare aquarium set on the counter so a new fish cannot dirty every tank in the shop. Sandboxing gives that fish its own water, not the hose that feeds the rare collection. Sandboxing does not mean the new fish is friendly; it means the mess stays in one glass. Sandboxing still needs the filter cleaned, because a sick fish can ruin the water it was given. Sandboxing is not a sensor for a smashed lid, not a padlock on the shop door, and not a truck sent to empty every tank. Sandboxing proof is a dye drop in the spare tank that never appears in the rare collection. Sandboxing fails when someone runs a tube from the spare tank into the main line for convenience.',
        'r4': 'Sandboxing: open risky attachments only in the confined viewer. Sandboxing: keep a canary file outside the box and try it. Sandboxing: list the paths and calls the box may use. Sandboxing: patch escape defects quickly and leave the box on. Sandboxing: refuse exceptions that grant the entire home directory. Sandboxing: separate this control from wipe, locks, and jailbreak checks. Sandboxing: record denied opens during investigations. Sandboxing: reset kiosk sandboxes at logoff.',
        'exam': "Sandboxing items describe confinement of a program or a document, proven when a sample cannot read outside its grant. Sandboxing is not the label for a rooted phone, a PIN plus ciphertext, or a console erase. Sandboxing that mounts the person's whole document folder is confinement in name only. Sandboxing still sits beside patching rather than instead of it.",
    },
    'Procurement': {
        'body': "Procurement is the path by which the organization buys or subscribes, and security requirements belong in that path before the purchase order is signed. Procurement should demand that default passwords be changed, that unused services be disabled, and that the supported lifetime be long enough to patch. Procurement reviews of a camera, a badge reader, or a cloud tool should ask where data will sit, who administers it, and how it is turned off at the end. Procurement blocks surprise cloud signups by requiring a known account and a security review instead of a credit card in someone's drawer.\n\nProcurement language can require a vulnerability-disclosure contact, a software bill of materials, and a patch service-level for anything that will touch production. Procurement is not the act of labeling an asset after it arrives, and it is not the inventory row itself. Procurement is not the discovery scan that later finds the device the purchase never recorded. Procurement records should include the security checklist, the approver, and the exception if a requirement was waived.\n\nProcurement of open-source support is still a purchase decision when the organization relies on a maintainer or a paid distribution. Procurement should refuse products whose only administration channel is an obsolete clear-text protocol. Procurement for services needs a data-processing description, a subprocessors list, and an exit plan that returns or deletes data. Procurement timing matters because a clinic that buys first and asks security later inherits the vendor's defaults.\n\nProcurement pilots should use non-production data unless a signed review says otherwise. Procurement of renewed contracts is another chance to add missing requirements, not a rubber stamp of last year's terms. Procurement evidence on a ticket is the requirement in the order and the acceptance test that showed the default password was gone. Procurement coordination with legal and finance keeps security from being a surprise invoice or an unenforceable wish.\n\nProcurement standards can tier reviews so a cheap mouse does not take the same weeks as a payroll host, while anything networked still answers a short question set. Procurement should name an internal owner before the vendor's salesperson becomes the de facto owner. Procurement of hardware for resale or donation later should already say how media will be sanitized. Procurement exceptions for an urgent clinical device still need a written expiry and a compensating barrier.\n\nProcurement training for buyers stops the pattern where a department creates a tenant over a weekend. Procurement is effective when a sample order shows the security lines and the received device matches them. Procurement questionnaires should ask whether the product can disable unused listeners, export logs, and run without a shared local administrator. Procurement of a medical device should ask how long the vendor will ship security fixes after sale and what happens when that window ends.\n\nProcurement language can require the vendor to notify the organization of a vulnerability under a defined clock rather than at the vendor's leisure. Procurement reviews of open-source components inside a commercial product belong in the same checklist as the chassis, because the buyer inherits those components. Procurement should demand a configuration guide that starts from a hardened baseline rather than from an open demo. Procurement of identity-integrated tools should ask which protocols are supported and whether older clear-text binds can be turned off.\n\nProcurement acceptance can include a scan of the delivered image so a surprise listener is caught before production. Procurement files should keep the security checklist beside the contract, not in a personal folder that vanishes when the buyer changes jobs. Procurement of a renewal is the moment to remove a waiver that was meant to last one year and quietly lasted three. Procurement scoring can weight security answers so a cheap bid with no patch story loses to a bid that can be maintained.\n\nProcurement of cloud regions should state where backups are copied, not only where the primary console sits. Procurement stakeholders include the future operator, because a product security cannot administer will be administered badly. Procurement of trial tenants must expire, with data deleted, or the trial becomes an unmanaged production shadow. Procurement records of waived password rules should point at the compensating barrier that was promised in the same document.\n\nProcurement training uses one example of a bad camera buy and one example of a clean buy so new buyers see the difference. Procurement is stronger when the receiving dock will not tag an asset whose acceptance test is still blank.",
        'r0': 'Procurement puts security requirements into the buy itself: supported lifetime, no default passwords, disabled unused services, and a way to get patches. Procurement for a cloud service adds data location, subprocessors, and an exit that deletes or returns data. Procurement should stop unreviewed tenant signups. Procurement records the checklist and any waived line with an approver. Procurement is the purchase path, distinct from later classification, from the inventory row, and from a discovery scan. Procurement acceptance tests show the received product met the security lines on the order. Procurement checklists should travel with the contract and survive a buyer changing jobs. Procurement renewals are a second chance to drop a waiver that outlived its reason. Procurement acceptance can include a scan of the image that actually arrived.',
        'r1': 'Procurement puts security into the purchase. Procurement asks for patches and a supported life. Procurement forbids leaving default passwords. Procurement reviews cloud tools before signup. Procurement requires an internal owner. Procurement records any waived requirement. Procurement is not the inventory list. Procurement is not a sensitivity label. Procurement is not a discovery scan. Procurement of renewals can add missing terms. Procurement exceptions need an end date. Procurement proof is the order plus a test. Procurement asks how long fixes will ship. Procurement keeps the checklist with the contract.',
        'r2': 'Procurement is the laboratory that orders glassware only from a written spec, including lids that actually seal. Procurement refuses a pretty flask that arrives with a hole where the stopper should be. Procurement also refuses a free sample cart that a rep wheels in without the spec. Procurement writes the spec number on the receipt and notes any seal that was waived for a week. Procurement is not the tag that later names who tends the flask, and not the shelf list, and not the walk-through that finds an extra flask. Procurement of a refill order is a fresh chance to demand the seal that was missing last time. Procurement proof is a received flask whose stopper matches the spec, not a smile from the vendor.',
        'r4': 'Procurement: put security lines on the order before signature. Procurement: require default passwords to be changed before acceptance. Procurement: name an internal owner in the request. Procurement: block unreviewed cloud signups. Procurement: record any waived line with an expiry. Procurement: test one received device against the order. Procurement: add missing terms at renewal. Procurement: keep urgent buys time-boxed and fenced.',
        'exam': 'Procurement items look for security language in the buy, before the asset exists on a shelf. Procurement is correct when the order demands a changed default password and a patch path. Procurement is not the label for a sensitivity marking added later. Procurement is not the label for the master list of what is already owned. Procurement waivers without an end date are the sloppy answer.',
    },
    'Ownership and classification': {
        'body': "Ownership and classification give every asset a named person and a sensitivity marking so handling is not a matter of taste. Ownership and classification make the owner accountable for access decisions, for accepting risk, and for saying when the asset may be retired. Ownership and classification use the marking to drive encryption, sharing, retention, and who may see a copy. Ownership and classification fail when the named owner has left and nobody inherited the row.\n\nOwnership and classification fail when everything is marked with the highest label, because staff then ignore the mark. Ownership and classification of a payroll database should show a restricted marking and a current owner inside the inventory, not only in someone's memory. Ownership and classification is not the purchase path that brought the database in. Ownership and classification is not the inventory mechanism itself, though the inventory is where the owner and the mark are stored.\n\nOwnership and classification should distinguish the data owner, who decides purpose, from a custodian who runs backups. Ownership and classification changes when a project ends or a system is merged, and the mark should be reviewed rather than copied forever. Ownership and classification evidence is a row with a person, a label, and a date the label was confirmed. Ownership and classification labels should be few enough to teach, with examples of what each label allows.\n\nOwnership and classification for a shared folder means the folder has one owner even if many people contribute files. Ownership and classification disputes go to the owner, not to the loudest requester in chat. Ownership and classification of logs and backups follows the data they contain, or a loose backup becomes the easiest copy. Ownership and classification training stops people from pasting restricted rows into an unapproved chat.\n\nOwnership and classification reviews each year catch owners who changed jobs. Ownership and classification is weak when a marking exists on paper and the access control list was never adjusted to match. Ownership and classification for vendors should say whether the vendor is a custodian and which mark they may process. Ownership and classification does not by itself discover shadow systems; it labels what the organization has agreed to recognize.\n\nOwnership and classification of a test system must say whether production data is forbidden there. Ownership and classification documentation includes the label names, example data, and the owner role description. Ownership and classification of a data set should name a deputy so leave and illness do not freeze access decisions. Ownership and classification labels need handling examples: who may mail the file, whether it may sit on a laptop, and whether a vendor may see it.\n\nOwnership and classification of derived data, such as a dashboard extract, follows the highest mark inside the extract unless a real de-identification review says otherwise. Ownership and classification arguments about a shared drive are settled by the owner, and the access list is then edited to match, not the other way around. Ownership and classification of log data is easy to under-mark because logs feel operational, yet authentication logs can be as sensitive as the accounts they describe. Ownership and classification reviews should sample folders where the mark says restricted and the permission says everyone in the domain.\n\nOwnership and classification changes when a system moves from a pilot with synthetic rows to a pilot with real rows, and the mark must move the same day. Ownership and classification for a vendor-hosted system still names an internal owner; the vendor is not the owner by default. Ownership and classification documentation should say how a new label is requested so teams do not invent private marks. Ownership and classification of printed output matters in clinics where a restricted record can sit on a tray.\n\nOwnership and classification inheritance in a document system should be tested, because a parent folder mark that does not reach a child file is a false comfort. Ownership and classification of credentials and keys is its own class of asset, with a tighter owner set than the application they unlock. Ownership and classification meetings once a year are enough only when joiners and leavers already trigger updates. Ownership and classification evidence for an audit is the row, the matching permission sample, and the date the owner last confirmed both.\n\nOwnership and classification of a shared mailbox still names one accountable person even when many people can send.",
        'r0': 'Ownership and classification assign a named accountable person and a sensitivity label to an asset. Ownership and classification let the label drive sharing, storage, and retention, while the owner accepts risk and approves unusual access. Ownership and classification should separate that owner from the custodian who operates backups. Ownership and classification rows live in the inventory and go stale when the person leaves. Ownership and classification is not the buying process and not the act of discovering an unknown host. Ownership and classification proof is a current name plus a label that matches the access people actually have. Ownership and classification of an extract follows the highest mark in the source unless a real review says otherwise. Ownership and classification needs a deputy so decisions survive vacation.',
        'r1': 'Ownership and classification names a person and a label. Ownership and classification makes the owner accountable. Ownership and classification uses the label to set handling. Ownership and classification includes backups of that data. Ownership and classification is not the purchase step. Ownership and classification is not the asset list itself. Ownership and classification separates owners from custodians. Ownership and classification goes stale when people leave. Ownership and classification should match real access. Ownership and classification forbids production data on casual test boxes. Ownership and classification needs a yearly check. Ownership and classification proof is the current row.',
        'r2': 'Ownership and classification is a tree tag in an orchard that names the steward and how precious the fruit is. Ownership and classification tells pickers which baskets may leave the gate. Ownership and classification rots when the steward moves away and the tag still bears that name. Ownership and classification fails if every tree is tagged precious, because pickers stop reading tags. Ownership and classification is not the order that bought the sapling and not the ledger that merely counts trees. Ownership and classification of a crate of picked fruit follows the tree, or the crate becomes an unlabeled side door. Ownership and classification proof is a tag whose steward still answers and whose basket rules match the tag.',
        'r4': 'Ownership and classification: name a living owner on every row. Ownership and classification: apply a label and match access to it. Ownership and classification: include backups under the same label. Ownership and classification: separate the owner from the custodian. Ownership and classification: reassign rows when people leave. Ownership and classification: forbid production extracts on unlabeled test hosts. Ownership and classification: review labels yearly. Ownership and classification: keep the label set small enough to teach.',
        'exam': 'Ownership and classification items require both a named accountable person and a sensitivity mark. Ownership and classification is not the purchase checklist and not the mere existence of a list. Ownership and classification is weak when the mark never changes who can open the folder. Ownership and classification of backups follows the source data. Ownership and classification with a departed owner is an incomplete row.',
    },
    'Inventory': {
        'body': "Inventory is the maintained list of hardware, software, and data the organization actually intends to manage. Inventory is the reason a patch cycle can find a host, and a host missing from the list is a host missing from the patch cycle. Inventory rows should carry owner, location or system, version or model, and the classification already agreed. Inventory is not the same as a one-time discovery scan, though discovery is how lies in the list are found.\n\nInventory is not the purchase order, which may describe what was requested rather than what still runs. Inventory of data stores matters as much as inventory of laptops, because a forgotten file share holds the same records as the primary application. Inventory updates belong in the change path so a new server is listed before it is exposed. Inventory accuracy can be sampled by comparing a subnet discovery to the rows and explaining every extra or missing address.\n\nInventory of installed software, including libraries inside images, feeds the later question of which flaws apply. Inventory should mark assets that are decommissioned so a stale row does not look like a live gap. Inventory evidence for a finding is the missing row, the extra live host, or a version that does not match the row. Inventory tools drift, and a console that has not reported for a month is not an inventory.\n\nInventory of cloud accounts needs the same discipline, or a tenant created for a demo never enters the list. Inventory ownership is the list steward, distinct from each asset's owner, and someone must chase blank fields. Inventory of shadow spreadsheets is uncomfortable and still necessary when those sheets are the real process. Inventory retention of old rows supports investigations without pretending the asset still runs.\n\nInventory is the wrong place to hide an exception; the exception belongs in the risk record, while the asset remains visible. Inventory reviews before a penetration test or a major change stop the embarrassment of an unknown exposed service. Inventory fields for serial numbers tie later disposal certificates back to a real device. Inventory of containers and serverless functions counts as software inventory even when there is no chassis to barcode.\n\nInventory communication should tell staff how to request a row rather than plugging in an unlisted appliance. Inventory is effective when a random sample of live addresses is explained by the list and a random sample of rows is still powered or logically present. Inventory of network ranges should state which team may add a subnet, because an unlisted subnet never receives an agent. Inventory reconciliation each month should explain unknown DHCP leases rather than deleting the report.\n\nInventory of virtual machines includes the image name and the hypervisor cluster, not only an IP address that will change. Inventory of certificates and of domain names belongs beside server inventory, or expirations surprise the same team that claims the list is complete. Inventory fields for business purpose stop a row that says only Linux from hiding whether the box is payroll or a sandbox. Inventory of end-of-life software is a valid row with a flag, not a row people delete to make the report look healthy.\n\nInventory imports from the purchasing system are a feed, and they still need a human to close the loop when a device is returned. Inventory of containers should key off the image digest, because a mutable tag can hide a changed build. Inventory access should be broad enough for operators to read and narrow enough that random edits do not rewrite owners. Inventory of data stores includes the backup target, or the list describes the front door and forgets the copy.\n\nInventory sampling can pick ten serials from the loading dock and ten addresses from a switch table. Inventory errors are corrected in the list, not by silencing the discovery tool that found them. Inventory of loaner equipment needs a temporary owner and a return date so the row does not fossilize. Inventory standards should forbid duplicate serials, which usually mean a process recorded the box twice or cloned an identifier.\n\nInventory of retired rows keeps the serial for disposal matching and clearly marks that the asset is not in service. Inventory is the list a new analyst can trust on day one without an oral history from the previous analyst. Inventory of spare parts in a closet counts when those parts can be deployed without a new purchase.",
        'r0': 'Inventory is the living list of hardware, software, and data stores the organization claims to manage. Inventory rows need an owner, a version or model, a place, and a classification. Inventory feeds patching, because unlisted hosts are skipped by habit. Inventory is updated through change, and discovery is the comparison that exposes omissions. Inventory is not the purchase path and not the sensitivity decision by itself, though it stores that decision. Inventory of cloud tenants and container images counts even without a metal serial plate. Inventory proof is a sample where live systems and rows explain each other. Inventory of images should key off a digest, not a mutable tag. Inventory of unknown DHCP leases needs an explanation, not a deleted report.',
        'r1': 'Inventory lists hardware, software, and data. Inventory is what patching depends on. Inventory rows need an owner and a version. Inventory should include cloud tenants. Inventory is not a one-time discovery scan. Inventory is not the purchase order. Inventory is not the sensitivity decision alone. Inventory must be updated when systems change. Inventory goes stale if agents stop reporting. Inventory serials later match disposal records. Inventory samples compare live hosts to rows. Inventory hides nothing that is still running. Inventory includes certificate and name records. Inventory flags end-of-life software instead of hiding it.',
        'r2': "Inventory is the orchestra library's catalog of scores, stands, and borrowed instruments. Inventory tells the librarian what to repair before the tour, and an unlisted stand is a stand nobody repairs. Inventory is not the receipt from the shop that sold a stand, and not the tag that says who leads a section. Inventory is checked by walking the stage and comparing what is present to the cards. Inventory of rented scores counts, even though no warehouse shelf holds them overnight. Inventory fails when cards remain for instruments that were sold and omits the new drum that arrived Friday. Inventory proof is a walk where every sound on stage has a card and every card still has an instrument.",
        'r4': 'Inventory: record hardware, software, and data stores. Inventory: fill owner, version, place, and label. Inventory: add a row before a system is exposed. Inventory: compare discovery results to the list. Inventory: include cloud tenants and images. Inventory: retire rows when assets are gone. Inventory: sample a subnet against the rows. Inventory: keep serials for later disposal matching. Inventory: explain unknown leases instead of deleting them.',
        'exam': "Inventory items ask whether a maintained list explains the live hardware, software, and data. Inventory is correct when a missing row explains a missed patch. Inventory is not the buyer's checklist and not the sensitivity label by itself. Inventory that has not been compared to a discovery pass is a wish. Inventory of a forgotten test server is the classic miss.",
    },
    'Enumeration': {
        'body': 'Enumeration is the disciplined look at what is really present on networks and in accounts, used to test whether the inventory is telling the truth. Enumeration finds hosts, services, and identities that nobody listed, such as a webcam plugged in by a project that ended. Enumeration is authorized discovery with a scope, a time, and an owner, not a freelance scan from a laptop on a guest network. Enumeration results must be compared to the inventory row by row, and every extra or missing item needs a sentence.\n\nEnumeration of accounts finds dormant logins and groups that still grant rights after a contract ended. Enumeration is not sanitization, which removes data from media. Enumeration is not physical destruction of a disk. Enumeration is not the certificate that says disposal happened.\n\nEnumeration is not the retention schedule that says how long records stay. Enumeration evidence is the unlisted address, the unexpected open service, or the account that should have been disabled. Enumeration frequency should match how fast the environment changes, with cloud accounts checked more often than a locked wiring closet. Enumeration credentials, when used, should be a dedicated read-only path so the check does not become an admin backdoor.\n\nEnumeration output belongs in the same ticket as the inventory correction, or the list and the scan drift apart again. Enumeration of wireless names and of exposed management pages is in scope when those are assets the organization claims to control. Enumeration should avoid disruptive tests; a discovery of a service is not permission to exploit it. Enumeration after a merger is how two partial lists become one honest list.\n\nEnumeration communication tells site staff the window so a medical device is not surprised by a probe. Enumeration is effective when a previously unknown device is either added to the inventory with an owner or removed from the network. Enumeration of management interfaces should note products that still answer on a legacy protocol the standard forbids. Enumeration of cloud security groups can reveal a world-open path that the inventory diagram does not draw.\n\nEnumeration of service accounts should list where the password or key is stored, because an account with no owner is a finding of its own. Enumeration schedules in plants and clinics should avoid shift changes when a probe could be mistaken for a device failure. Enumeration results that repeat for three cycles without a list correction are a process failure, not a scanning failure. Enumeration of wireless identifiers and of captive portals belongs in the same pass when those networks carry staff traffic.\n\nEnumeration notes should separate a newly found asset from an asset that was known and merely unpatched. Enumeration handoff to the list steward should include the evidence file, not a verbal count of surprises.',
        'r0': 'Enumeration compares live hosts, services, and accounts with the inventory under an authorized scope. Enumeration turns an unlisted webcam or a dormant login into a correction, not into a exploit attempt. Enumeration output should land in the same record as the inventory edit. Enumeration is distinct from wiping media, shredding it, certifying disposal, and setting a retention clock. Enumeration credentials ought to be dedicated and limited. Enumeration proof is an extra or missing item that the list then explains. Enumeration of security groups can show a path the diagram omits. Enumeration that repeats the same surprise for three cycles is a broken process. Enumeration notes should separate a new asset from a known asset that is merely unpatched. Enumeration evidence files should travel with the list correction.',
        'r1': 'Enumeration checks what is actually present. Enumeration compares results to the inventory. Enumeration needs an authorized scope. Enumeration finds unlisted hosts and old accounts. Enumeration is not media wiping. Enumeration is not shredding. Enumeration is not a disposal certificate. Enumeration is not a retention schedule. Enumeration should not become an exploit. Enumeration results must update the list. Enumeration of mergers needs a fresh pass. Enumeration proof is the unexplained extra item. Enumeration flags legacy management protocols. Enumeration checks cloud paths against diagrams. Enumeration separates new assets from old ones.',
        'r2': "Enumeration is a keeper in a skiff counting buoys against last year's chart. Enumeration marks a buoy that is in the water and missing on the chart, and a chart mark with no buoy. Enumeration does not scrub paint off a buoy, smash a buoy, file a wreck form, or decide how many years a logbook stays on the shelf. Enumeration is done on purpose in daylight, not as a joyride. Enumeration ends when the chart is corrected or the stray buoy is pulled. Enumeration proof is the mismatch written in the margin, then cleared. Enumeration of a second pass still only corrects the chart. Enumeration does not repaint a buoy or decide its retirement date.",
        'r4': 'Enumeration: scope the look and name an owner. Enumeration: compare every hit to the inventory. Enumeration: record unlisted hosts and dormant accounts. Enumeration: use a limited credential if one is required. Enumeration: keep probes from becoming exploits. Enumeration: tell sites the window. Enumeration: correct the list or remove the stray. Enumeration: separate this pass from wipe, shred, and retention. Enumeration: attach the evidence file to the list correction.',
        'exam': 'Enumeration items are about discovering reality and correcting the list. Enumeration is not wiping, shredding, a disposal certificate, or a keep-or-delete schedule. Enumeration without a scope is the reckless distractor. Enumeration ends when the unlisted thing is owned on the list or gone from the network. Enumeration without an evidence file is unfinished.',
    },
    'Sanitization': {
        'body': "Sanitization removes data from media so the next holder cannot read it, using a method matched to how sensitive the data was. Sanitization is not deleting a shortcut, emptying a recycle bin, or formatting that leaves contents recoverable with common tools. Sanitization for magnetic disks may be a full overwrite or a purge method, while many solid-state devices are better cleared by a cryptographic erase that destroys the media key. Sanitization must follow the sensitivity, so a public kiosk image and a payroll laptop do not automatically take the same procedure.\n\nSanitization is distinct from physical destruction, which is the path when the drive will not complete a wipe or the data is too sensitive to leave the media intact. Sanitization is distinct from the certificate that records the event, though the wipe should produce evidence the certificate can cite. Sanitization is distinct from enumeration, which only finds systems, and from retention, which decides when deletion is due. Sanitization of phones and laptops should use the platform's supported erase and then verify that a sample file path no longer returns the old data.\n\nSanitization before resale, donation, or lease return is the usual business trigger. Sanitization of a failed drive that cannot take commands is no longer sanitization; it becomes destruction. Sanitization logs should name the asset serial, the method, the tool version, and the person. Sanitization of cloud volumes means deleting the volume and the snapshots and the replica, or the data survives beside the wiped disk.\n\nSanitization standards should say which methods are acceptable for each classification. Sanitization is incomplete when the backup cartridge that holds the same records is ignored. Sanitization testing can recover a lab disk after a weak method to show why that method is banned. Sanitization of copier and printer disks is in scope when those devices stored scans.\n\nSanitization does not classify the data and does not decide the purchase. Sanitization of virtual disks must include snapshots the hypervisor kept for rollback, or the next restore publishes the old files. Sanitization of a laptop should also clear the management enrollment so the device does not phone home as if it were still employed. Sanitization choices for classified or regulated sets should be the purge or destroy end of the scale, not a single overwrite chosen because it was fast.\n\nSanitization tools need a version pin, because an old build may claim success on media it no longer understands. Sanitization of encrypted drives still needs a key-destruction step when the key was escrowed, or the escrow copy remains a reader. Sanitization records should be created by the tool, not typed from memory an hour later. Sanitization of a phone trade-in uses the vendor erase and then a check that the previous account is gone from the setup screen.",
        'r0': "Sanitization makes stored data unrecoverable by a later holder, with the method chosen from the data's sensitivity. Sanitization rejects shortcut deletion and casual format as inadequate. Sanitization of many solid-state drives is a cryptographic erase of the media key, while magnetic media may be overwritten. Sanitization stops when the device cannot accept the command, and destruction takes over. Sanitization must include snapshots and backups that would restore the same bits. Sanitization evidence is the method, serial, tool, and operator, ready for the disposal record. Sanitization is not enumeration and not the retention clock. Sanitization of virtual disks includes snapshots, and sanitization of escrowed keys includes the escrow copy. Sanitization tools should be version-pinned so a success message is meaningful.",
        'r1': 'Sanitization removes data from media. Sanitization is more than deleting an icon. Sanitization matches the method to sensitivity. Sanitization may overwrite magnetic disks. Sanitization may crypto-erase solid-state keys. Sanitization includes backups and snapshots. Sanitization stops if the drive will not run. Sanitization then hands off to destruction. Sanitization logs the serial and the method. Sanitization is not a discovery scan. Sanitization is not the retention rule. Sanitization is not the paper certificate by itself. Sanitization includes hypervisor snapshots. Sanitization also clears escrowed keys. Sanitization tools should be version-pinned.',
        'r2': "Sanitization is a print shop washing plates so the next customer cannot pull yesterday's poster from them. Sanitization is not brushing crumbs of ink onto the floor and calling the plate clean. Sanitization of a plate that still shows the image under a rinse needs a harder wash, matched to how private that poster was. Sanitization of a cracked plate that will not sit in the press is no longer a wash. Sanitization also washes the proof sheets stored in the drawer, or the image remains. Sanitization is not counting plates, not smashing them, not the receipt, and not the rule for how long proofs are kept. Sanitization proof is a washed plate that will not print the old job.",
        'r4': 'Sanitization: pick a method that matches the data mark. Sanitization: do not accept a shortcut delete as complete. Sanitization: crypto-erase solid-state media when that is the supported path. Sanitization: include backups and snapshots. Sanitization: log serial, method, tool, and operator. Sanitization: switch to destruction if the device will not take commands. Sanitization: verify a sample path no longer returns old files. Sanitization: cover copier and phone storage, not only laptops.',
        'exam': 'Sanitization items require data to be made unreadable to the next holder, not merely hidden. Sanitization is not counting assets, not smashing media, and not the signed disposal record. Sanitization that skips backups leaves a full copy behind. Sanitization of a dead drive that cannot run a wipe is the cue to switch methods.',
    },
    'Destruction': {
        'body': 'Destruction physically ruins media so data cannot be recovered when a wipe is impossible or not trusted for the sensitivity involved. Destruction methods include shredding, pulverizing, incinerating, and, for magnetic media, degaussing that leaves the disk unusable. Destruction of solid-state media should not rely on degaussing, because those chips are not magnetic platters and a magnet does not erase them. Destruction is the right path for a failed drive that will not accept sanitization commands.\n\nDestruction is paired with a certificate when a vendor does the work, and the serials must match. Destruction is not the logical wipe itself. Destruction is not the act of discovering the drive. Destruction is not the retention rule that said the data should go.\n\nDestruction in-house needs a witnessed procedure and a bin that is not left in a public hall. Destruction particle size or method should be defined in the standard so a coarse smash that leaves readable chips is not accepted. Destruction of paper follows the same idea with cross-cut shredding or pulping when the mark requires it. Destruction evidence is the destroyed serial, the method, the date, and the witness or vendor.\n\nDestruction scheduling should be soon after decommission, not after a year in a closet of intact disks. Destruction is excessive for empty media that never held sensitive data, where a documented sanitization may be enough. Destruction contracts must forbid the vendor from reselling intact disks. Destruction is complete when a sampled serial from the batch appears on the destruction record and the media is no longer readable.\n\nDestruction batches should be small enough that a missing serial is obvious before the truck leaves. Destruction of mixed media in one bin is a mistake when the standard requires different methods for platters and for chips. Destruction witnesses should be someone other than the person who packed the bin, so the record is not a single point of trust. Destruction of paper and of disks can share a vendor and still needs separate lines on the form.\n\nDestruction schedules after a breach may be faster than the ordinary quarterly run, and the reason should be on the ticket. Destruction of tokens and of smart cards follows the same serial discipline as drives. Destruction leftovers, such as a tray of screws and labels, should not include a readable board. Destruction vendors should allow a spot check of particle size or of a pulverized sample.\n\nDestruction in a satellite clinic needs a locked holding container so drives do not sit under a desk until the quarterly pickup. Destruction photos, if used, should show the serial and the ruined media in the same frame.',
        'r0': 'Destruction physically ruins disks, chips, or paper when sanitization cannot be trusted or cannot run. Destruction by shredding, pulverizing, or incineration fits solid-state and failed drives. Destruction by degaussing fits magnetic media and does not erase flash chips. Destruction should happen promptly and produce serial-level evidence. Destruction is not enumeration, not a logical wipe, not the retention schedule, and not the certificate text by itself, though the certificate records it. Destruction contracts must ban resale of intact media. Destruction batches should be small enough that a missing serial is obvious. Destruction witnesses should not be the same person who packed the bin. Destruction of chips and of platters should not share one vague method line. Destruction holding containers at small sites must lock.',
        'r1': 'Destruction physically ruins media. Destruction is for drives that cannot be wiped. Destruction includes shredding and pulverizing. Destruction by degaussing fits magnetic disks only. Destruction does not degauss flash chips. Destruction needs serials and a witness. Destruction is not a logical wipe. Destruction is not a discovery scan. Destruction is not a retention clock. Destruction is not the certificate wording alone. Destruction should happen promptly. Destruction contracts forbid reselling intact disks. Destruction batches stay small enough to audit. Destruction uses a second witness. Destruction keeps chips and platters distinct.',
        'r2': 'Destruction is the kiln that slumps a contaminated mold so no one can cast the old shape again. Destruction is chosen when washing the mold is impossible or not trusted. Destruction of a clay mold is heat and collapse, not a magnet waved over a metal shelf. Destruction is not counting molds, not a rinse, not the date on a storage rule, and not the receipt that merely says the kiln was hot. Destruction happens before the mold spends a year on an open rack. Destruction proof is a slumped lump whose serial was written down, not a mold that still casts.',
        'r4': 'Destruction: choose it when a wipe cannot finish or is not enough. Destruction: shred or pulverize flash rather than degaussing it. Destruction: degauss only magnetic media. Destruction: record serial, method, date, and witness. Destruction: do not leave intact disks in a closet queue. Destruction: forbid vendors from reselling intact media. Destruction: match particle size to the standard. Destruction: separate this step from the retention rule and the discovery pass.',
        'exam': 'Destruction items mean physical ruin of media, especially when a wipe cannot run. Destruction is not degaussing a solid-state drive. Destruction is not the discovery of the drive and not the schedule that said to delete files. Destruction without serials cannot be tied to the asset later. Destruction that leaves readable chips is incomplete.',
    },
    'Certification of disposal': {
        'body': "Certification of disposal is the filed proof that a wipe or a physical ruin actually took place, naming the asset, the method, the date, and the party who did the work. Certification of disposal is what lets a ticket close, because a verbal claim that a closet was emptied is not evidence. Certification of disposal must be matched to serial numbers or asset tags before the record is accepted. Certification of disposal from a recycler should be checked against the shipping list so a drive that never left the building cannot hide under a batch certificate.\n\nCertification of disposal is not the wipe or the shred itself; it is the proof those actions happened. Certification of disposal is not enumeration and not the retention schedule. Certification of disposal should be stored where investigators and auditors can retrieve it for the required period. Certification of disposal gaps are findings even when the media really was destroyed, because the organization cannot show it.\n\nCertification of disposal for in-house shredding can be a witnessed log if the standard allows, with the same fields a vendor form would carry. Certification of disposal quality includes method and particle size or wipe standard, not only a logo at the top of a page. Certification of disposal of cloud data may be an export of the provider's deletion confirmation tied to the account and the date. Certification of disposal training stops staff from tossing disks and filing nothing.\n\nCertification of disposal is effective when a random disposed serial can be found on a certificate and the method matches policy. Certification of disposal numbers should be unique per batch so two events cannot be confused in an audit. Certification of disposal scans of the paper form should be readable, because an unreadable page is not a record. Certification of disposal exceptions, such as a drive destroyed in a fire, still need a narrative, a date, and the evidence that remains.\n\nCertification of disposal linkage to the ticket system lets a later reader move from a serial to the story without a private spreadsheet. Certification of disposal of leased equipment should satisfy the lessor and the internal standard, and the stricter of the two wins. Certification of disposal delays are visible when the asset state says disposed and no proof is attached after a defined number of days. Certification of disposal training for the loading dock stops a well-meant recycle drop that produces no names.\n\nCertification of disposal retention follows the rule for audit evidence, which may outlive the media itself. Certification of disposal quality reviews sample a batch each quarter and try to trace one serial both directions.",
        'r0': 'Certification of disposal documents who sanitized or destroyed which serial, by which method, and on which date. Certification of disposal is matched to the asset list before a ticket closes. Certification of disposal is the record, not the shredder and not the wipe tool. Certification of disposal from a vendor must reconcile to the drives that actually shipped. Certification of disposal is distinct from finding assets, from choosing how long to keep data, and from performing the destruction. Certification of disposal is weak when the form lacks a method or a serial. Certification of disposal needs readable pages and unique batch numbers. Certification of disposal of a fire loss still needs a narrative. Certification of disposal that is missing after the asset is marked gone should alarm.',
        'r1': 'Certification of disposal is the proof record. Certification of disposal names serial, method, date, and party. Certification of disposal is matched before a ticket closes. Certification of disposal is not the shredder itself. Certification of disposal is not the wipe tool itself. Certification of disposal is not a discovery scan. Certification of disposal is not a retention schedule. Certification of disposal must reconcile vendor batches. Certification of disposal includes cloud deletion confirmations. Certification of disposal without serials fails. Certification of disposal is stored for later review. Certification of disposal can be an in-house witnessed log.',
        'r2': 'Certification of disposal is the archive form that says a damaged reel was destroyed, by whom, and on which day. Certification of disposal is checked against the reel numbers on the shipping crate. Certification of disposal is not the act of melting the reel and not the count of reels on the shelf. Certification of disposal is not the rule for how long a healthy reel stays in the vault. Certification of disposal with a pretty letterhead and no reel numbers does not close the file. Certification of disposal lets a later researcher prove the reel is gone. Certification of disposal proof is a form whose numbers match the crate and whose method matches the house rule.',
        'r4': 'Certification of disposal: require serial, method, date, and party. Certification of disposal: reconcile vendor forms to the shipping list. Certification of disposal: refuse forms that lack serials. Certification of disposal: file the record where audits can find it. Certification of disposal: accept in-house logs only with a witness. Certification of disposal: tie cloud deletion notices to the account. Certification of disposal: do not close the ticket on a verbal claim. Certification of disposal: separate the record from the shred and from retention.',
        'exam': 'Certification of disposal items ask for proof that wipe or destruction happened, matched to serials. Certification of disposal is not the discovery pass and not the keep-or-delete schedule. Certification of disposal is not satisfied by a logo with no asset identifiers. Certification of disposal closes the loop after sanitization or destruction, rather than replacing either action.',
    },
    'Data retention': {
        'body': 'Data retention states how long each kind of record is kept and when it must be deleted, including the copies that would bring it back. Data retention is not a slogan that everything should be kept forever in case it is useful. Data retention schedules name the record type, the start of the clock, the duration, and the deletion method. Data retention must cover backups, replicas, and mail archives, or a restore on Monday resurrects a file deleted on Friday.\n\nData retention yields to a legal hold, which pauses deletion for named matters and must be lifted when the matter ends. Data retention is distinct from enumeration, sanitization technique, physical destruction, and the disposal certificate, though deletion at the end of the clock uses those techniques. Data retention that is shorter than a regulation is a compliance miss, and retention that is far longer is a privacy and breach-cost miss. Data retention of logs should be long enough for investigations and no longer than the schedule allows.\n\nData retention owners approve exceptions in writing with an end date. Data retention implementation in the application is a feature, not a yearly manual purge that people skip. Data retention evidence is the schedule plus a sample of records older than the limit that are actually gone, including from backups. Data retention communication tells staff not to keep private shadow copies in uncontrolled drives.\n\nData retention for a departed product includes the tenant data, not only the marketing site. Data retention is effective when a dated test record disappears on schedule and cannot be restored from the usual backup. Data retention clocks should name the event that starts them, such as account closure or the last transaction, because an undefined start never rings. Data retention of chat and of ticket comments is easy to forget when the policy only mentions the file share.\n\nData retention conflicts between two rules, such as a short privacy limit and a longer financial limit, are resolved in writing before the system is configured. Data retention in a data lake must delete derived tables, not only the source file that fed them. Data retention tests should restore a backup taken before the delete and show that the aged record is removed again by the next job, or the restore becomes a bypass. Data retention of research data may be long and still finite, with a review rather than an immortal archive.\n\nData retention notices to customers should match the real schedule, or the notice is a separate defect. Data retention ownership for each line of the schedule is a named role, reviewed when that role changes hands.',
        'r0': 'Data retention sets a duration and a deletion point for each record type, and it includes backups that would undo the delete. Data retention is not indefinite storage by default. Data retention pauses for a specific legal hold and resumes when the hold lifts. Data retention that ignores replicas leaves a second copy alive. Data retention uses sanitization or destruction at the end, but it is the clock and the rule, not those techniques. Data retention proof is a record past its date that is gone from the primary store and from the restore path. Data retention exceptions need an owner and an end. Data retention clocks need a defined start event. Data retention of derived tables must follow the source delete.',
        'r1': 'Data retention sets how long data stays. Data retention also sets when deletion is due. Data retention includes backups and replicas. Data retention is not keep-everything forever. Data retention pauses for a legal hold. Data retention resumes when the hold ends. Data retention is not a discovery scan. Data retention is not the wipe technique itself. Data retention is not physical shredding itself. Data retention is not the disposal certificate. Data retention needs an owner for exceptions. Data retention proof is a record that actually ages out.',
        'r2': 'Data retention is the date stamped on a film can that says when that reel must leave the vault. Data retention also pulls the duplicate reel from the second vault, or the date is theater. Data retention is not a rule that every reel stays because film feels historical. Data retention can pause for a lawsuit about one title, then start again when the case ends. Data retention is not counting cans, washing a print, melting a print, or the form that proves the melt. Data retention proof is a can past its date that is gone from both vaults. Data retention fails when editors keep a private copy in a desk that the stamp never reaches.',
        'r4': 'Data retention: write duration and start-of-clock per record type. Data retention: include backups, replicas, and archives. Data retention: implement deletion in the system, not as a skipped yearly chore. Data retention: pause only for a named legal hold. Data retention: lift the hold when the matter ends. Data retention: forbid endless shadow copies. Data retention: test that an aged record cannot be restored. Data retention: separate the schedule from wipe technique and from certificates.',
        'exam': 'Data retention items set a keep period and a real deletion, including backups. Data retention is not enumeration, sanitization technique, shredding, or the disposal certificate. Data retention that keeps everything forever is the common wrong answer. Data retention under a legal hold is a pause, not a new forever. Data retention fails if a restore brings the record back.',
    },
    'Vulnerability scans': {
        'body': "Vulnerability scans look for known flaws and weak settings by comparing a target to signatures and configuration checks. Vulnerability scans are not exploitation, and a finding is not by itself proof that an attacker can reach the flaw from the internet. Vulnerability scans may be unauthenticated, seeing only what a remote party would see, or credentialed, logging in to read patch levels and local settings the banner hides. Vulnerability scans from an agent on the host can see software the network path cannot.\n\nVulnerability scans need a scope, a schedule, and a window so a clinical device is not probed during a procedure. Vulnerability scans produce candidates for triage, including false alarms where a banner never changed after a patch. Vulnerability scans miss issues that have no signature yet, which is why they do not replace design review or application testing. Vulnerability scans of a lab printer and of an internet-facing application are both findings, and they are not equal priorities.\n\nVulnerability scans should be tuned so safe checks are the default and disruptive checks are opt-in. Vulnerability scans evidence on a ticket includes the plugin or check identifier, the host, the port or service, and whether the scan was credentialed. Vulnerability scans after a change confirm whether a fix worked, which is a later verification step rather than the scan's identity. Vulnerability scans are not static reading of source code and are not dynamic testing that sends crafted inputs to a running application.\n\nVulnerability scans of containers should see the image contents, not only the orchestrator address. Vulnerability scans credentials must be vaulted and limited, or the scanner becomes the most dangerous account in the fleet. Vulnerability scans should cover new subnets quickly, because an unscoped acquisition is where unknown services hide. Vulnerability scans differ from a penetration test, which attempts to exploit under rules of engagement.\n\nVulnerability scans reporting to leadership should be summarized, while operators keep the raw finding list. Vulnerability scans frequency for internet-facing systems is higher than for isolated lab gear. Vulnerability scans that are never compared to the asset inventory will skip hosts the scanner cannot route to and still look complete. Vulnerability scans of default credentials and clear-text management protocols are configuration findings, still in scope.\n\nVulnerability scans do not assign a public identifier by themselves and do not compute business impact, though both may be attached later. Vulnerability scans are effective when credentialed coverage is high, new assets appear within the schedule, and findings are tracked to a decision. Vulnerability scans of authenticated Windows and Linux hosts should read the actual package database rather than trusting a banner that an administrator can edit. Vulnerability scans of network devices need a read-only account that can show the running image version.\n\nVulnerability scans schedules should stagger sites so a single scanner does not saturate a thin clinic link. Vulnerability scans of web applications inside the same program are easy to double-count with dynamic tests, and the ticket should say which tool produced the row. Vulnerability scans exclusions for a fragile device must name the device, the reason, and a compensating monitor, or the exclusion becomes a permanent blind spot. Vulnerability scans plugins should be updated before the scan, and the plugin set version belongs in the report header.\n\nVulnerability scans of IPv6 ranges fail when the scanner only walks IPv4, so dual-stack sites need an explicit check. Vulnerability scans through a firewall must be allowed on purpose, or the report describes the firewall rather than the hosts. Vulnerability scans of default community strings and of default local accounts are configuration findings worth keeping even when no CVE is attached. Vulnerability scans trend lines matter: a flat critical count can hide one team fixing issues while another team deploys new ones.\n\nVulnerability scans of golden images should happen before the image is cloned, because every clone inherits the finding. Vulnerability scans credentials that expire mid-quarter silently drop authenticated coverage, so the expiry should alarm before the password dies. Vulnerability scans of ephemeral cloud instances need an agent or a snapshot in time, or the instance vanishes and the finding vanishes with it. Vulnerability scans handoff to owners should include a reproduction note a system administrator can follow without relearning the scanner.\n\nVulnerability scans of printer fleets and of voice gateways are in scope when those devices sit on routable networks. Vulnerability scans quality reviews ask what percentage of inventory rows were actually touched, not how many pages the PDF contained.",
        'r0': 'Vulnerability scans compare systems to known flaw and configuration checks without requiring the operator to exploit the target. Vulnerability scans see more when they are credentialed or agent-based than when they only touch the network banner. Vulnerability scans are candidates for triage, not automatic proof of external reachability. Vulnerability scans need scope, schedule, and safe-check defaults. Vulnerability scans are distinct from static code reading and from dynamic input testing of a running application. Vulnerability scans also differ from a penetration test that exploits under a written agreement. Vulnerability scans proof is a check identifier on a named host with the authentication mode recorded. Vulnerability scans should record the plugin-set version in the header. Vulnerability scans exclusions need a named device and a reason, or they become blind spots.',
        'r1': 'Vulnerability scans search for known flaws and weak settings. Vulnerability scans are not exploitation. Vulnerability scans can be credentialed or not. Vulnerability scans with a login see more. Vulnerability scans need a scope and a schedule. Vulnerability scans can false-alarm on banners. Vulnerability scans miss brand-new flaws. Vulnerability scans are not static code reading. Vulnerability scans are not dynamic app probing. Vulnerability scans are not a penetration test. Vulnerability scans must cover new assets. Vulnerability scans produce findings to triage.',
        'r2': 'Vulnerability scans are a bridge inspector with a checklist walking each span on a set day. Vulnerability scans note cracks that match the pictures in the manual. Vulnerability scans do not load the span until it breaks, and a noted crack is not proof a truck can reach it from the highway. Vulnerability scans with a key to the maintenance hatch see more than a look from the riverbank. Vulnerability scans are not the architect reading drawings at a desk and not a wind-tunnel shove of a model. Vulnerability scans of a footbridge and of the main span both get notes, and the town fixes the main span first. Vulnerability scans proof is a checklist line tied to a specific span, not a shrug that bridges feel old.',
        'r4': 'Vulnerability scans: set scope, schedule, and safe checks. Vulnerability scans: prefer credentialed or agent coverage. Vulnerability scans: record the check, the host, and the auth mode. Vulnerability scans: vault the scanner credential. Vulnerability scans: add new subnets to scope quickly. Vulnerability scans: do not treat a finding as permission to exploit. Vulnerability scans: separate these results from code review and from live app tests. Vulnerability scans: compare coverage to the asset inventory.',
        'exam': 'Vulnerability scans items describe signature and configuration checks, not an exploit and not proof of reach. Vulnerability scans are not static reading of source and not crafted inputs against a running app. Vulnerability scans with credentials see a different depth than a banner-only pass. Vulnerability scans that skip new networks create a false sense of coverage. Vulnerability scans feed triage rather than replacing it.',
    },
    'Static application testing': {
        'body': "Static application testing reads source code or a built binary without executing the application, looking for dangerous patterns before the program is live. Static application testing is the short name SAST in many build pipelines, and the idea is the same: inspection of the artifact at rest. Static application testing can flag a query built by concatenating user input, a hardcoded secret, an unsafe deserialization call, or a dependency with a known bad version if the scanner understands the manifest. Static application testing runs naturally in continuous integration so a failing check blocks the merge.\n\nStatic application testing does not see configuration that exists only on the live host, and it does not see flaws that appear only when two services interact at runtime. Static application testing often raises findings that a human must confirm, because a pattern match is not always a reachable bug. Static application testing of a binary without source is weaker and still useful when the vendor shipped object code only. Static application testing rules should be tuned per language so a flood of style nits does not hide an injection issue.\n\nStatic application testing differs from a banner check aimed at a live server. Static application testing is not dynamic application testing, which runs the program and sends inputs. Static application testing and dynamic application testing overlap on some bug classes and miss different ones, so a mature pipeline uses both rather than arguing for a single winner. Static application testing evidence is the file, the line or binary offset, the rule identifier, and the build number.\n\nStatic application testing suppressions need an owner and a reason, or developers silence the scanner until it reports nothing. Static application testing should run on pull requests and again on the release branch so a direct commit cannot skip it. Static application testing secrets findings should point at rotation, not only at deleting the line, because the secret may already have been copied. Static application testing of infrastructure-as-code belongs in the same family when the template can be evaluated without deploying it.\n\nStatic application testing performance in huge monorepos may need incremental scans, but the release build should still be complete. Static application testing does not assign a CVE by itself, though a result may cite one. Static application testing does not calculate business impact. Static application testing false negatives include logic bugs that look like ordinary code and flaws in generated code the scanner never parsed.\n\nStatic application testing integration with the ticketing system should open one item per confirmed issue, not one email per raw hit. Static application testing baselines on legacy code can stop the scan from failing forever on old debt, while new code stays strict. Static application testing of mobile clients and of server code are both in scope if the organization ships both. Static application testing training helps developers read a finding instead of treating the tool as an adversary.\n\nStatic application testing is effective when a seeded bug in a test repository is caught and when a known-safe pattern is not permanently suppressed by habit. Static application testing of pull requests should comment on the changed lines first so authors are not buried under legacy debt. Static application testing of generated code should either scan the generator's templates or explicitly accept that the generated output is unreviewed. Static application testing rules for secrets should understand common token shapes without flagging every long hex string in a test fixture.\n\nStatic application testing severity maps should be local, so a tool's critical label is translated into the team's rubric before a build fails. Static application testing of infrastructure templates can catch an open security group before anyone applies the template. Static application testing baselines must be approved by a person, and new findings after the baseline still fail the build. Static application testing in a monorepo should attribute a finding to the team that owns the directory.\n\nStatic application testing false-alarm reviews once a quarter delete suppressions that no longer match the code. Static application testing of third-party source that is vendored into the tree is in scope, not only first-party files. Static application testing logs should keep the rule version so a later reader knows which definition fired. Static application testing does not excuse a missing unit test for a business rule the pattern matcher cannot see.\n\nStatic application testing rollout to a noisy legacy repo can start in report-only mode for a fixed period and then block.",
        'r0': 'Static application testing inspects source or a binary without running it, which is what teams mean by SAST. Static application testing catches patterns such as concatenated queries, hardcoded secrets, and unsafe calls at build time. Static application testing misses purely runtime configuration and many multi-service flaws. Static application testing findings need human confirmation because pattern matches include false alarms. Static application testing is not a host vulnerability scan and is not dynamic testing that sends live inputs. Static application testing suppressions require an owner. Static application testing proof is a rule hit tied to a file and a build. Static application testing baselines need a human approval, and new hits after that baseline still fail the build. Static application testing should attribute a hit to the directory owner.',
        'r1': 'Static application testing reads code without running it. Static application testing is also called SAST. Static application testing can flag injection patterns. Static application testing can flag hardcoded secrets. Static application testing belongs in the build. Static application testing misses some runtime flaws. Static application testing raises false alarms. Static application testing is not a host scan. Static application testing is not dynamic testing. Static application testing suppressions need an owner. Static application testing proof is file, rule, and build. Static application testing pairs well with live tests.',
        'r2': 'Static application testing is an architect reading blueprints at a desk before anyone pours concrete. Static application testing marks a beam drawn where a doorway must be, while the building is still paper. Static application testing cannot feel a draft that appears only when the doors are hung and the wind arrives. Static application testing sometimes circles a mark that a senior builder knows is a symbol, not a mistake, so a person still decides. Static application testing is not a clipboard walk on the finished bridge and not a model shoved into a wind tunnel. Static application testing of a suppressed warning with no signature becomes a habit of ignoring the page. Static application testing proof is a circled line on a named sheet in a named revision.',
        'r4': 'Static application testing: run it on pull requests and on the release branch. Static application testing: block the merge on confirmed high findings. Static application testing: require an owner for every suppression. Static application testing: rotate secrets the scan exposes. Static application testing: tune rules per language. Static application testing: keep a seeded bug that must fail the build. Static application testing: do not call this a host vulnerability scan. Static application testing: keep dynamic testing as a separate stage.',
        'exam': 'Static application testing items, often called SAST, inspect code or binaries at rest. Static application testing is not a credentialed host scan and is not DAST against a running service. Static application testing that only runs on laptops and not in the build is easy to skip. Static application testing suppressions without owners are the usual failure. Static application testing and dynamic testing miss different bugs, so one does not retire the other.',
    },
    'Dynamic application testing': {
        'body': "Dynamic application testing runs the application and sends inputs, watching responses for flaws that exist only in motion. Dynamic application testing is the short name DAST when the target is a running build in a safe environment rather than production traffic. Dynamic application testing can show that a crafted search returns another person's row, that an error leaks a stack trace, or that an access check fails when the user changes an identifier. Dynamic application testing needs a deployed instance, test accounts, and permission to send hostile-looking but bounded inputs.\n\nDynamic application testing does not read every line of source, so a bug in a path the crawler never visits stays invisible. Dynamic application testing complements static application testing: one reads the artifact, the other exercises behavior. Dynamic application testing differs from a host check that only matches patch levels, though both can be scheduled. Dynamic application testing authenticated mode sees pages a stranger cannot, which is where many authorization bugs live.\n\nDynamic application testing unauthenticated mode mimics an outsider and should not be the only mode. Dynamic application testing scope must exclude production payment or clinical actions unless a separate exercise says otherwise. Dynamic application testing evidence is the request, the response, the account used, and the route. Dynamic application testing false alarms include scanners that misread a security header or call a logout page a flaw.\n\nDynamic application testing false negatives include business-logic abuse that no generic payload understands. Dynamic application testing of APIs needs the specification or a recorded session, or the scanner only hits the front door. Dynamic application testing rate should be limited so the tool does not become a denial of service against the test environment. Dynamic application testing findings should be reproduced by a person before a ticket is called confirmed.\n\nDynamic application testing after a fix means replaying the same request and showing the row or the trace is gone. Dynamic application testing credentials belong in a vault, and the test accounts should hold synthetic data. Dynamic application testing coverage metrics, such as routes visited, stop the team from trusting a scan that only loaded the home page. Dynamic application testing does not by itself name a CVE and does not set the organization's risk tolerance.\n\nDynamic application testing in the pipeline can run on each release candidate, with a longer crawl nightly. Dynamic application testing of mobile back ends uses the same idea against the API the app calls. Dynamic application testing reports should separate informational headers from exploitable access-control failures. Dynamic application testing is effective when a seeded authorization bug is found and when a fixed bug stays fixed on the next run.\n\nDynamic application testing accounts should be synthetic personas with realistic roles, including one low-privilege user and one administrator, so access-control gaps between them are visible. Dynamic application testing of single-page applications needs a crawler that executes scripts, or the scanner records only the empty shell. Dynamic application testing headers and tokens should be refreshed during a long crawl so an expired session is not mistaken for a locked-down application. Dynamic application testing of file upload routes belongs in scope, with samples that are harmless and clearly marked.\n\nDynamic application testing environments should mirror production headers and parsers closely enough that a finding is believable, while holding no real patient or card data. Dynamic application testing tickets should include the parameter that was changed, because an identifier swap is a different bug from a reflected script. Dynamic application testing schedules after a major dependency bump catch regressions the static stage may not execute. Dynamic application testing of rate-limited logins should back off, or the tool locks the test account and then reports a false outage.\n\nDynamic application testing scope files should list hosts that are forbidden so a misconfiguration cannot wander into production. Dynamic application testing results that repeat across releases without an owner are a queue problem, not a tool problem. Dynamic application testing of error pages should check that stack traces and query text are absent. Dynamic application testing authentication setup is part of the control, and a scan that never logged in should be marked partial.\n\nDynamic application testing correlation with a code line is a bonus when symbols exist, and it is not required to file the behavioral bug. Dynamic application testing cleanup should remove data the crawl created so the next run starts from a known store.",
        'r0': 'Dynamic application testing exercises a running application with crafted inputs, which is DAST against a safe instance. Dynamic application testing can reveal broken access checks, data leaks, and verbose errors that source inspection may not prove reachable. Dynamic application testing misses paths it never calls and misses many bugs that static review would see in unused code. Dynamic application testing should run authenticated and unauthenticated. Dynamic application testing is not a banner-based host scan and is not SAST. Dynamic application testing proof is a request and response pair tied to a route and an account. Dynamic application testing replays that pair after a fix. Dynamic application testing of a single-page application needs a crawler that runs scripts. Dynamic application testing that never logged in should be marked partial.',
        'r1': 'Dynamic application testing runs the application. Dynamic application testing sends crafted inputs. Dynamic application testing is also called DAST. Dynamic application testing needs a safe instance. Dynamic application testing can show broken access checks. Dynamic application testing misses paths it never hits. Dynamic application testing pairs with static testing. Dynamic application testing is not a host banner scan. Dynamic application testing should use test accounts. Dynamic application testing must be reproduced by a person. Dynamic application testing replays the same request after a fix. Dynamic application testing tracks which routes were visited.',
        'r2': "Dynamic application testing is a model airplane in a wind tunnel, with smoke and sensors, before any passenger boards. Dynamic application testing pushes the model through air and watches a wing flex in a way the drawing did not show. Dynamic application testing never sees a crack inside a part the smoke does not reach. Dynamic application testing is not the architect's desk review and not a checklist walk on a public bridge. Dynamic application testing uses a model, not the Saturday flight with a full cabin. Dynamic application testing writes the dial reading next to the angle of the wing. Dynamic application testing after a repair puts the same model back in the tunnel at the same angle.",
        'r4': 'Dynamic application testing: point it at a safe build, not at live clinical actions. Dynamic application testing: provide authenticated and outsider accounts. Dynamic application testing: record request, response, route, and account. Dynamic application testing: reproduce the finding before filing it as confirmed. Dynamic application testing: replay the same request after the fix. Dynamic application testing: measure which routes were visited. Dynamic application testing: vault the test credentials. Dynamic application testing: keep static testing as a separate stage.',
        'exam': 'Dynamic application testing items, often called DAST, require a running app and crafted inputs. Dynamic application testing is not SAST and is not a credentialed patch scan of a host. Dynamic application testing in production, against real patient actions, is the dangerous distractor. Dynamic application testing without route coverage can look finished after loading one page. Dynamic application testing confirmation is a repeated request, not a screenshot alone.',
    },
    'Package monitoring': {
        'body': "Package monitoring watches libraries and packages the organization already uses and alerts when a newly announced flaw matches one of those versions. Package monitoring reads lockfiles, image manifests, and dependency graphs, including transitive packages the developers did not type by hand. Package monitoring is what fires the morning a library flaw is published and the billing service still vendors that version. Package monitoring is not a live host scan, though a host scan may later confirm the package is loaded.\n\nPackage monitoring is not a threat feed of malicious addresses, and it is not a review of public job posts. Package monitoring should name the package, the version, the advisory, and the applications that include it. Package monitoring of container base images matters because the vulnerable bits may sit under the application code. Package monitoring noise drops when versions are pinned and when the graph is generated from the build rather than from a guess.\n\nPackage monitoring must see private registries, or an internal fork hides from the public advisory match. Package monitoring severity is an input, and reachability inside the app still decides urgency. Package monitoring exceptions for a package that cannot move this week need an owner and a date. Package monitoring should distinguish a build-time tool from a library shipped to customers.\n\nPackage monitoring evidence is the advisory identifier, the version range, and the repo or image that contains the hit. Package monitoring after a bump means the next build no longer resolves to the bad version. Package monitoring fails open when a team vendors a zip file that never enters the manifest. Package monitoring of firmware and of phone apps is the same idea with a different manifest.\n\nPackage monitoring cadence is continuous, because advisories do not wait for a quarterly change window. Package monitoring ownership sits with the team that ships the app, with a central view so executives see aging exceptions. Package monitoring does not by itself test the running request path; that remains dynamic testing or a host scan. Package monitoring of license changes can ride along, but a license surprise is not a vulnerability finding.\n\nPackage monitoring integration should open a ticket once per package per application, not a mail storm per developer. Package monitoring baselines help a new repository start clean without hiding later advisories. Package monitoring is effective when a deliberately old test dependency produces an alert the same day the advisory is added to the feed the tool uses. Package monitoring of transitive dependencies should show the parent that pulled them in, so the fix is a direct bump or an override rather than a guess.\n\nPackage monitoring ignore files are as sensitive as scanner suppressions and need an expiry. Package monitoring of language ecosystems differs: a lockfile in one ecosystem is a hash pin, and a range specifier in another will float. Package monitoring should fail a release when a forbidden severity is newly present, while older debt can be a timed exception. Package monitoring of container bases should rebuild on a cadence even when application code did not change, because the base moves.\n\nPackage monitoring advisories that are withdrawn should close the ticket automatically so teams do not chase a retracted issue. Package monitoring of development-only packages can be a lower tier when those tools never ship, and the tier must be explicit. Package monitoring reachability analysis, when the tool offers it, is a hint, not a proof, until someone confirms the vulnerable function is callable. Package monitoring across microservices needs a portfolio view so the same library bump is coordinated rather than done twelve times in twelve ways.\n\nPackage monitoring of firmware images for appliances belongs if the vendor publishes a version the tool can compare. Package monitoring during an incident can answer quickly whether a named package is present, which is a different question from the daily hygiene queue. Package monitoring mirrors should be the source of truth the build uses, or the monitor and the build disagree. Package monitoring of a forked library should track the fork's commits, not only the upstream advisory that the fork may never merge.\n\nPackage monitoring reports to leadership work as aging of exceptions, not as a raw count of transitive rows. Package monitoring onboarding of a new repository is incomplete until the first build produces a graph. Package monitoring of a bill of materials export should match the graph the build already produced.",
        'r0': 'Package monitoring matches announced flaws to the libraries and images the organization builds, including transitive dependencies. Package monitoring depends on real manifests and lockfiles rather than a remembered list. Package monitoring names the version, the advisory, and the affected application. Package monitoring is not a malicious-address feed and not a public-source review of job posts. Package monitoring urgency still depends on whether the vulnerable code is reachable. Package monitoring proof is a hit that disappears from the next build after the version bump. Package monitoring misses zip files and forks that never enter the graph. Package monitoring ignore files need an expiry, just as suppressions do. Package monitoring of a fork should follow the fork, not only the upstream notice. Package monitoring mirrors must match the build or the two stories diverge.',
        'r1': 'Package monitoring watches libraries you already use. Package monitoring reads lockfiles and image manifests. Package monitoring includes transitive dependencies. Package monitoring alerts when a new flaw matches. Package monitoring names the version and the app. Package monitoring is not a malicious-address feed. Package monitoring is not a review of public posts. Package monitoring is not a live host scan. Package monitoring still needs a reachability decision. Package monitoring exceptions need an owner and a date. Package monitoring must see private registries. Package monitoring is checked by a deliberate old dependency.',
        'r2': 'Package monitoring is a pharmacy watching lot numbers on the shelves against the morning recall board. Package monitoring includes the ingredient hidden inside a compound, not only the jar the pharmacist typed. Package monitoring rings when a recalled lot is actually on site. Package monitoring is not a list of suspicious street addresses and not a tourist brochure that reveals the shop layout. Package monitoring of a jar that was poured into an unlabeled tin fails, because no lot number remains. Package monitoring after a swap shows the new lot on the board and the old lot gone. Package monitoring proof is a recall line tied to a real jar, then a shelf that no longer holds it.',
        'r4': 'Package monitoring: generate graphs from builds, not from memory. Package monitoring: include transitive dependencies and base images. Package monitoring: alert with package, version, advisory, and app. Package monitoring: include private registries. Package monitoring: time-box exceptions with an owner. Package monitoring: bump the version and confirm the next build is clean. Package monitoring: do not treat a zip drop outside the manifest as invisible on purpose. Package monitoring: separate this watch from address feeds and from public-post reviews.',
        'exam': 'Package monitoring items match a new library flaw to a version the organization already ships. Package monitoring is not a feed of bad network addresses and not open-source intelligence about public posts. Package monitoring without transitive dependencies understates exposure. Package monitoring that cannot see a private registry is blind there. Package monitoring closure is a clean build, not a promise to upgrade later.',
    },
    'Threat feeds': {
        'body': "Threat feeds are streams of indicators, such as addresses, domains, URLs, and file hashes, that a defender can use to detect or block. Threat feeds are useful only with a judgment about false-alarm rate, age, and whether the indicator matches this environment. Threat feeds can arrive as a commercial subscription or as a community share, and the redistribution rules of the share still apply. Threat feeds that are dumped straight into a block list can break a business partner when a shared host or a content-delivery address is listed.\n\nThreat feeds should record why an indicator was trusted, when it expires, and which control consumes it. Threat feeds are not package monitoring, which watches your libraries, and they are not open-source intelligence gathered from public documents. Threat feeds of file hashes help endpoint tools, while address feeds help firewalls and resolvers, and mixing those types in one control is a parsing mistake. Threat feeds need a review when the source suddenly grows tenfold, because quantity is not quality.\n\nThreat feeds evidence on a ticket is the indicator, the source, the sighting time, and the local log line that matched. Threat feeds should be tested with a canary indicator the team controls, so a broken ingest is noticed. Threat feeds aging policy removes stale entries that would otherwise block an address reused by an innocent tenant. Threat feeds do not declare an incident by themselves; a match is a lead until a person or a correlated log agrees.\n\nThreat feeds for phishing URLs change quickly and need a shorter lifetime than a hash of a stable tool. Threat feeds integration should survive a source outage by keeping the last good set for a defined time, then alarming. Threat feeds sensitivity differs for a clinic that blocks aggressively and a lab that only alerts. Threat feeds must not include indicators the organization is forbidden to redistribute into a public ticket.\n\nThreat feeds of command names that are too generic, such as a common shell, create noise rather than detection. Threat feeds ownership includes someone who can pause a bad source the same day. Threat feeds comparison across two sources helps when one is noisy and the other is slow. Threat feeds are effective when a planted test indicator alerts or blocks, and when a known-benign partner address is not stuck on the list after its listing expires.\n\nThreat feeds of IP addresses should be matched with care on shared hosting and on content-delivery networks, where one address serves many customers. Threat feeds of domains can be safer to block than addresses when the domain is attacker-controlled and the address is not. Threat feeds ingested in STIX or a simple CSV still need a mapping so a hash is not loaded into an address list. Threat feeds overlap should be measured: two sources that always agree add little, and two that never agree need an explanation.\n\nThreat feeds used for mail filtering should not include the organization's own sending domains. Threat feeds consumer systems should log the source identifier on every block so a later outage can be traced to a bad entry. Threat feeds review meetings can retire a source whose confirmations stay near zero for a quarter. Threat feeds of hashes for commodity tools will collide with administrator software if the hash is too generic a utility.\n\nThreat feeds timing for a fast phishing campaign is hours, not the weekly change window used for firewall hygiene. Threat feeds legal terms may forbid sending the indicators into a ticketing tool that contractors can read. Threat feeds enrichment with local asset ownership turns a bare address into a decision. Threat feeds of TOR exit nodes or similar broad categories are policy choices, not automatic malice.\n\nThreat feeds testing in a lab should show both a hit and a non-hit before production enforcement. Threat feeds rollback plans matter when a bad publish blocks a critical partner. Threat feeds documentation lists the consumer, the action, the expiry default, and the person who can pause the source. Threat feeds are a supplement to local detection content the team writes from its own incidents.\n\nThreat feeds paused for a partner outage should record who paused them and when they must be restored. Threat feeds of newly registered domains can be noisy and still useful as an alert rather than as a hard block.",
        'r0': 'Threat feeds supply indicators such as addresses, domains, URLs, and hashes for detection or blocking. Threat feeds require a measured false-alarm rate, an expiry, and a named consumer. Threat feeds can disrupt partners if a shared address is blocked without review. Threat feeds are not library monitoring and not research in public documents. Threat feeds matches are leads until local evidence agrees. Threat feeds need a canary to prove ingest works and an aging rule so stale entries leave. Threat feeds proof is a sourced indicator tied to a local log line. Threat feeds on shared hosting addresses need extra care before a block. Threat feeds should log the source identifier on every enforced hit. Threat feeds rollback must exist before enforcement, because a bad publish will eventually arrive.',
        'r1': 'Threat feeds stream indicators for detect or block. Threat feeds include addresses, domains, URLs, and hashes. Threat feeds need an expiry and a source. Threat feeds can false-alarm. Threat feeds can break partners if applied blindly. Threat feeds are not library monitoring. Threat feeds are not public-document research. Threat feeds matches are leads, not automatic incidents. Threat feeds need a canary test. Threat feeds must age out stale entries. Threat feeds should pause a bad source quickly. Threat feeds proof is a local log match.',
        'r2': 'Threat feeds are the harbor notices that list shoals reported by pilots this week. Threat feeds help the next boat only if the notice says who reported the shoal and how old the report is. Threat feeds that nail a permanent chain across the whole channel will also stop the ferry. Threat feeds are not a recall board of medicine lots and not a tourist map of the town. Threat feeds of a shoal that was dredged last season should come off the board. Threat feeds proof is a notice plus a sounding from your own boat, not the notice alone. Threat feeds fail when every scrap of gossip is carved into the board with no date.',
        'r4': 'Threat feeds: record source, indicator type, and expiry. Threat feeds: measure false alarms before a hard block. Threat feeds: separate address lists from file hashes. Threat feeds: plant a canary and confirm ingest. Threat feeds: age out stale indicators. Threat feeds: pause a source that suddenly floods. Threat feeds: treat a match as a lead until local logs agree. Threat feeds: keep redistribution limits in mind.',
        'exam': 'Threat feeds items are streams of indicators used with judgment, not automatic guilt. Threat feeds are not package monitoring and not open-source intelligence. Threat feeds dumped into a permanent block list are the risky distractor. Threat feeds without expiry accumulate stale damage. Threat feeds value shows up when local evidence matches the indicator.',
    },
    'Open-source intelligence': {
        'body': "Open-source intelligence is what anyone can learn from public sources about the organization, its suppliers, and its exposed services. Open-source intelligence includes job postings that name the exact badge vendor, public code repositories, certificate-transparency entries, DNS records, and leaked credential dumps that were posted where the public can already read them. Open-source intelligence is a defensive activity here: see the organization as an outsider would, then reduce what is published. Open-source intelligence is not a threat feed you subscribe to, and it is not the monitor that matches library versions to advisories.\n\nOpen-source intelligence from a job ad that describes internal tool names gives a stranger a map of where to look, so hiring text should be reviewed. Open-source intelligence of an exposed management page is a finding about your own perimeter, discovered without special access. Open-source intelligence handling of leaked passwords means reset and investigation, not a write-up that repeats the passwords. Open-source intelligence collection should stay within public sources and the organization's own authorized views, without trespass or attempts to break authentication.\n\nOpen-source intelligence evidence is the public URL or document, the date it was seen, and the internal exposure it reveals. Open-source intelligence cadence can be a scheduled review of the organization's domains, certificates, and code hosts. Open-source intelligence false leads include a name collision with another company and an old document that no longer matches production. Open-source intelligence sharing inside the organization should omit anything that would multiply the exposure, such as reposting a live secret.\n\nOpen-source intelligence is not permission to attack a system that was found; it is permission to close or explain it. Open-source intelligence of merger news and facility addresses is often already public and still relevant to physical security planning. Open-source intelligence training for recruiters reduces the next overly specific job post. Open-source intelligence tools that query public certificate logs and public DNS are enough for a basic self-view.\n\nOpen-source intelligence tickets should name the owner who will change the posting, the repository, or the exposed service. Open-source intelligence does not replace internal scanning; it shows what was already visible outside. Open-source intelligence is effective when a seeded public test document is found by the review and then taken down or generalized. Open-source intelligence reviews of public code should search for the organization's domains, internal host names, and key headers that do not belong in a public commit.\n\nOpen-source intelligence of certificate-transparency logs can show a host name that was issued and never inventoried. Open-source intelligence of paste sites and public breach collections should be handled as leads, with resets rather than with copies stored in general chat. Open-source intelligence of facility photos on social networks can reveal badge printers, network closets, or door controls, and the response is physical as often as technical. Open-source intelligence of job posts should be sampled monthly by someone who did not write the post.\n\nOpen-source intelligence of supplier incidents is public news that still changes the organization's own risk, and it belongs in the vendor file. Open-source intelligence collection notes should record the query, because a later analyst needs to repeat it. Open-source intelligence of stale subdomains pointing at abandoned cloud buckets is a classic self-finding and a cleanup task. Open-source intelligence sharing with a peer group must respect that group's markings even though the original source was public.\n\nOpen-source intelligence tools that require an account should use a role account approved for the purpose, not a personal login. Open-source intelligence false positives from a common product name should be filtered with a second identifier such as a domain. Open-source intelligence of executive travel or home addresses is out of scope unless a specific protective duty exists, and even then it is tightly held. Open-source intelligence remediation of a public repository may mean making it private, rewriting history if a secret was committed, and rotating that secret.\n\nOpen-source intelligence cadence after a product launch should tighten for a few weeks when new host names are likely. Open-source intelligence reports should separate what the public can see from what the team infers, so inference is not written as fact. Open-source intelligence success is a removed detail or a closed service, measured a month later so the detail does not return. Open-source intelligence of a public bucket listing should be filed as an exposed store, with the bucket name and the date seen.",
        'r0': 'Open-source intelligence gathers what the public can already see: job posts, code, DNS, certificate logs, and exposed services. Open-source intelligence for a defender is a mirror, followed by reducing detail that helps an intruder. Open-source intelligence is not an address-block feed and not library version monitoring. Open-source intelligence stops at public information and authorized views, without breaking authentication. Open-source intelligence on leaked secrets leads to reset, not to republication. Open-source intelligence proof is a dated public source tied to an internal change. Open-source intelligence false leads include name collisions and stale pages. Open-source intelligence of certificate logs can reveal a host name nobody inventoried. Open-source intelligence notes should record the query so it can be repeated. Open-source intelligence inferences must be labeled as inferences, not as observed facts.',
        'r1': 'Open-source intelligence uses public sources. Open-source intelligence includes job posts and public code. Open-source intelligence includes DNS and certificate logs. Open-source intelligence includes your own exposed services. Open-source intelligence is a defensive mirror. Open-source intelligence is not a block-list feed. Open-source intelligence is not library monitoring. Open-source intelligence does not repeat live secrets. Open-source intelligence does not authorize breaking in. Open-source intelligence needs a dated source. Open-source intelligence should change the risky publication. Open-source intelligence can be fooled by stale pages.',
        'r2': "Open-source intelligence is a tourist map sold on the corner that accidentally draws the alley behind the shop and the side window. Open-source intelligence is the shopkeeper buying that map to see the alley, then moving the safe off the window line. Open-source intelligence is not the pilots' shoal board and not the pharmacy recall list. Open-source intelligence does not include picking the lock to check the alley; the map was already for sale. Open-source intelligence of a map from 1990 may show a wall that is now a kitchen, so the date matters. Open-source intelligence proof is the map page and the moved safe, not a stack of maps nobody reads. Open-source intelligence fails when the shop reprints the alley in its own brochure.",
        'r4': 'Open-source intelligence: review job posts, public repos, and certificate logs on a schedule. Open-source intelligence: treat exposed management pages as findings. Open-source intelligence: reset leaked credentials and do not republish them. Open-source intelligence: stay on public and authorized sources. Open-source intelligence: date every source. Open-source intelligence: assign an owner to change the publication or the service. Open-source intelligence: discard stale or wrong-organization hits. Open-source intelligence: keep this mirror separate from feeds and package graphs.',
        'exam': 'Open-source intelligence items use public information, such as a job post that names internal systems. Open-source intelligence is not a threat feed and not package monitoring. Open-source intelligence that includes breaking a login has left the concept. Open-source intelligence value is the change that reduces exposure. Open-source intelligence without a date cannot tell a current page from a dead one.',
    },
    'Information-sharing organizations': {
        'body': "Information-sharing organizations let peer organizations warn one another about campaigns, under rules about what may be repeated and to whom. Information-sharing organizations in a sector, often called an ISAC or a similar trust group, trade indicators and techniques with membership agreements. Information-sharing organizations use markings, such as traffic-light colors, to say whether a recipient may share further, and those markings are part of the data. Information-sharing organizations are not a dark-web monitoring product and not a mathematical false-alarm rate, though their tips can be wrong.\n\nInformation-sharing organizations require the team to use a received indicator without placing the sender's identity into an open channel. Information-sharing organizations work only if members also contribute, within what their counsel allows. Information-sharing organizations should name an internal point of contact and a backup so a warning does not sit in one inbox. Information-sharing organizations evidence is the received report, its marking, the date, and the internal action taken.\n\nInformation-sharing organizations handling rules belong in the incident procedure so a night responder does not paste a restricted indicator into a public ticket. Information-sharing organizations may provide context that a commercial feed lacks, because a peer describes the campaign and not only an address. Information-sharing organizations can be wrong or early, so a tip is verified against local logs before a major change. Information-sharing organizations membership lists are themselves sensitive.\n\nInformation-sharing organizations for a small clinic may be the sector group rather than a bespoke alliance. Information-sharing organizations legal review should bless the agreement before the first contribution of internal data. Information-sharing organizations metrics can track time from receipt to a local decision, not the raw count of emails. Information-sharing organizations are distinct from publishing a blog post about an incident.\n\nInformation-sharing organizations after a staffing change need the contact updated the same week. Information-sharing organizations tools for ingest should keep the marking attached to the indicator as it moves into detection content. Information-sharing organizations are effective when a marked exercise message is handled at the right sensitivity and produces a local check without leaking the sender. Information-sharing organizations onboarding should include a tabletop where a marked indicator arrives after hours and the responder handles it correctly.\n\nInformation-sharing organizations contribution review stops an eager analyst from sending internal victim names or regulated data into the group. Information-sharing organizations traffic-light habits should be taught with examples of what each color allows in this organization. Information-sharing organizations duplicates of a commercial feed are still worth having when the peer context explains a campaign the feed only lists as an address. Information-sharing organizations secure mail or portal should be the path, not a personal inbox that forwards to a contractor by habit.\n\nInformation-sharing organizations indicator quality feedback helps the group, and a note that an indicator was benign is a contribution. Information-sharing organizations membership fees and legal terms should be reviewed like any other vendor, because the data is sensitive. Information-sharing organizations contacts in the member directory should be role addresses that survive vacations. Information-sharing organizations during an incident can be asked for sector context, and the request itself must carry the right marking.\n\nInformation-sharing organizations records of what was shared outward are as important as what was received, for later regulatory questions. Information-sharing organizations conflicts, such as a tip that would require blocking a major partner, go to the incident lead rather than to an automatic control. Information-sharing organizations exercise injects should be labeled so nobody treats a drill as a live campaign. Information-sharing organizations value is lost when received indicators sit unparsed for weeks, so ingest time is a metric.\n\nInformation-sharing organizations sector peers may be competitors, and the agreement exists so that fact does not stop safety warnings. Information-sharing organizations withdrawal procedures should say what happens to indicators already received. Information-sharing organizations local detection content built from a tip should cite the marking in an internal field operators can see. Information-sharing organizations success in a quarter can be one campaign recognized earlier because a peer described it first.\n\nInformation-sharing organizations may publish a member code of conduct that forbids using a tip to gain a commercial advantage. Information-sharing organizations tools should reject an outbound paste that strips the marking. Information-sharing organizations annual reviews should ask whether the group still matches the sector the organization actually operates in. Information-sharing organizations success stories should name the campaign that was seen earlier, without naming the peer in public.",
        'r0': "Information-sharing organizations exchange warnings among peers under a membership agreement and redistribution markings. Information-sharing organizations make the marking travel with the indicator. Information-sharing organizations forbid casual public posting of a peer's identity. Information-sharing organizations tips still need a local check before disruptive action. Information-sharing organizations are not a criminal-market watch service and not the definition of false positives. Information-sharing organizations need a living contact and a night procedure that preserves markings. Information-sharing organizations proof is a marked report plus a local action that did not leak the source. Information-sharing organizations need an after-hours tabletop with a marked indicator. Information-sharing organizations should use role addresses, not a single personal inbox. Information-sharing organizations must record what the organization sends, not only what it receives.",
        'r1': "Information-sharing organizations let peers warn each other. Information-sharing organizations follow sharing rules. Information-sharing organizations use markings on indicators. Information-sharing organizations hide the peer's name in public. Information-sharing organizations expect members to contribute. Information-sharing organizations need a named contact. Information-sharing organizations tips can be wrong. Information-sharing organizations require a local check. Information-sharing organizations are not dark-web monitoring. Information-sharing organizations are not a false-alarm formula. Information-sharing organizations belong in the night procedure. Information-sharing organizations proof is marked handling. Information-sharing organizations drill after-hours handling. Information-sharing organizations record outbound shares too.",
        'r2': "Information-sharing organizations are a storm-net on the radio where neighboring harbors describe a squall, under a rule about who may repeat the call. Information-sharing organizations put a color on the call that says whether it may be relayed. Information-sharing organizations do not print the caller's name on a billboard. Information-sharing organizations still have someone look out this harbor's own window before moving every boat. Information-sharing organizations are not a pawn-shop watch for stolen violins and not a tally of false bells. Information-sharing organizations fail when the only radio sits with one person who is on leave. Information-sharing organizations proof is a colored note in the log and a local action that kept the caller's name off the pier chalkboard.",
        'r4': 'Information-sharing organizations: sign the agreement before contributing. Information-sharing organizations: keep markings attached to every indicator. Information-sharing organizations: name a contact and a backup. Information-sharing organizations: verify tips against local logs. Information-sharing organizations: ban public paste of peer names. Information-sharing organizations: update contacts when staff change. Information-sharing organizations: write the handling rule into the night procedure. Information-sharing organizations: drill with a marked exercise message.',
        'exam': "Information-sharing organizations items hinge on peer warnings plus redistribution rules. Information-sharing organizations are not dark-web monitoring and not the false-positive concept. Information-sharing organizations markings are not optional decoration. Information-sharing organizations that publish a peer's name have broken the control. Information-sharing organizations still require local verification. Information-sharing organizations drills must be labeled so a drill is not treated as live.",
    },
    'Dark-web monitoring': {
        'body': "Dark-web monitoring looks for the organization's data, brands, or credentials in criminal markets and similar hidden forums, usually through a specialist service rather than by staff browsing those markets. Dark-web monitoring treats a hit as a lead to verify, not as an automatic declaration that a breach occurred. Dark-web monitoring samples can be fabricated, recycled from an old public breach, or belong to a different organization with a similar name. Dark-web monitoring of a mail address should be checked against whether that address is real, whether the password works, and whether the source is new.\n\nDark-web monitoring is not the peer-sharing group and not the statistics of false positives and false negatives, though a hit can be either a false lead or a missed earlier clue. Dark-web monitoring procedures must tell staff not to buy stolen data and not to wander criminal forums with corporate accounts. Dark-web monitoring evidence is the report excerpt, the date, the indicator type, and the verification result. Dark-web monitoring verification that a password still works should be done carefully, preferably by a reset and a forced sign-in review rather than by typing the leaked password into production.\n\nDark-web monitoring of customer records requires the privacy response path, not only a technical reset. Dark-web monitoring frequency is set by the service contract, and gaps in the service should be visible. Dark-web monitoring false comfort comes from a vendor dashboard that stays green because the query is misspelled. Dark-web monitoring scope should include key domains and executive addresses without turning into surveillance of employee private lives beyond the work identifiers.\n\nDark-web monitoring matches that prove valid should open an incident or a credential-reset wave, depending on what was exposed. Dark-web monitoring matches that fail verification should be closed with the reason, so the next analyst does not repeat the panic. Dark-web monitoring legal advice matters before anyone stores criminal-market samples. Dark-web monitoring is not a substitute for patching or for access logs.\n\nDark-web monitoring communication to executives should explain that a mention is not yet a confirmed loss. Dark-web monitoring is effective when a planted, non-sensitive canary credential is reported by the service and the team verifies it without exposing real secrets. Dark-web monitoring vendor queries should be tested with a unique canary string that is not a real password and that staff can recognize. Dark-web monitoring of a hit that includes a password hash still needs a reset if the hash might be cracked offline by others, and the identity team should assume curiosity from the internet.\n\nDark-web monitoring scope lists should be reviewed when the organization acquires a domain, or the new name is invisible. Dark-web monitoring reports that only say the brand was mentioned, with no sample, are weak leads and should be ranked accordingly. Dark-web monitoring of customer email and password pairs is a privacy event path when the pairs check out as real. Dark-web monitoring staff access to the vendor portal should be few people, because the portal can contain other parties' data depending on the contract.\n\nDark-web monitoring false leads from a typo domain should be closed with the typo recorded so the next report is faster. Dark-web monitoring timing against access logs can show whether a leaked password was used, which changes a curiosity into an incident. Dark-web monitoring of executive accounts deserves a faster verification path than a generic mailbox. Dark-web monitoring contracts should say whether the vendor stores samples and for how long.\n\nDark-web monitoring is not a reason to skip multifactor enrollment, and a hit on a password is less damaging when a second factor held. Dark-web monitoring communications to a wide audience should avoid reproducing the sample. Dark-web monitoring of a recurring monthly hit on the same old breach can be suppressed only with a note that the source is unchanged and the passwords were rotated. Dark-web monitoring integration with the identity team should be a named queue, not an informal chat.\n\nDark-web monitoring of internal project names can reveal a leak of documents rather than of passwords, and the document owner then takes the lead. Dark-web monitoring metrics include time to verify, not the number of mentions, because mentions can be inflated by a chatty vendor. Dark-web monitoring after a real confirmation should feed lessons into how the data left, which may be phishing, a stolen laptop, or a vendor, and that cause is a separate investigation.",
        'r0': "Dark-web monitoring searches criminal markets and similar forums for the organization's data, usually through a specialist service. Dark-web monitoring output is a lead, verified before anyone declares a breach. Dark-web monitoring hits may be fake, old, or about another organization. Dark-web monitoring forbids staff from buying stolen data or browsing markets with work accounts. Dark-web monitoring is not peer information sharing and not the false-positive definition. Dark-web monitoring of a working credential leads to reset and investigation. Dark-web monitoring proof is a report plus a written verification result. Dark-web monitoring canaries should be unique strings that are not real passwords. Dark-web monitoring of a reused old breach can be quieted only after rotation is confirmed. Dark-web monitoring time-to-verify matters more than a raw mention count.",
        'r1': 'Dark-web monitoring searches criminal markets for your data. Dark-web monitoring usually uses a specialist service. Dark-web monitoring treats hits as leads. Dark-web monitoring does not auto-declare a breach. Dark-web monitoring hits can be fake or old. Dark-web monitoring must verify the sample. Dark-web monitoring forbids buying stolen data. Dark-web monitoring is not a peer-sharing group. Dark-web monitoring is not a false-alarm definition. Dark-web monitoring of real secrets triggers reset. Dark-web monitoring closes disproven hits with a reason. Dark-web monitoring is not a patch program.',
        'r2': "Dark-web monitoring is a violin shop that watches pawn listings for instruments stolen from its own studio. Dark-web monitoring treats a listing as a rumor until someone checks the serial and the varnish. Dark-web monitoring does not send the staff to buy the instrument in a back alley. Dark-web monitoring may find a photo of a student violin that was sold legally years ago. Dark-web monitoring is not the harbor storm-net and not a count of bad fire alarms. Dark-web monitoring of a matching serial locks the studio door's spare key and calls the insurer. Dark-web monitoring proof is the listing plus the serial check, written down either way.",
        'r4': 'Dark-web monitoring: use a specialist service rather than staff browsing markets. Dark-web monitoring: verify every hit before declaring a breach. Dark-web monitoring: reset real credentials instead of testing them casually. Dark-web monitoring: forbid purchasing stolen data. Dark-web monitoring: record the verification result. Dark-web monitoring: close false leads with a reason. Dark-web monitoring: include key domains without private-life surveillance. Dark-web monitoring: separate this lead source from peer-sharing rules.',
        'exam': 'Dark-web monitoring items are verified leads from criminal markets, not automatic breach declarations. Dark-web monitoring is not information-sharing organizations and not the false-positive concept. Dark-web monitoring that tells staff to buy a dump has left the control. Dark-web monitoring of an old or fake sample should close quietly with a reason. Dark-web monitoring of a still-valid secret becomes a reset and an investigation.',
    },
    'False positives and false negatives': {
        'body': "False positives and false negatives name two detector mistakes, and a program that tracks only one of them will lie. False positives and false negatives call the first mistake a signal that points at nothing harmful, for example a scanner that still cries about a library whose version banner stayed stale after the real update. False positives and false negatives call the second mistake a real defect the tool stayed quiet about, for example a host the scanner could not log into. False positives and false negatives both deserve a count, because crushing every false alarm by muting rules can manufacture the silent miss.\n\nFalse positives and false negatives show up in vulnerability scans, in mail filters, in intrusion signatures, and in application tests, and the arithmetic is the same. False positives and false negatives are not a peer-sharing club and not a criminal-market watch. False positives and false negatives handling for a banner-only hit is to confirm the patch level with a credentialed check or a local version read before closing the item as unreal. False positives and false negatives handling for a suspected miss is to ask which assets the tool cannot see and to sample them another way.\n\nFalse positives and false negatives rates should be computed on confirmed tickets, not on the raw scream of the tool. False positives and false negatives tuning needs a written reason when a rule is relaxed, plus a review date. False positives and false negatives evidence is the original alert, the confirmation note, and the label of unreal or missed. False positives and false negatives in a pilot of a new feed are the reason not to auto-block on day one.\n\nFalse positives and false negatives budgets can say how many analyst hours the noise may consume before the rule is redesigned. False positives and false negatives teaching helps new staff avoid two bad habits: closing everything as noise, or escalating everything as doom. False positives and false negatives after a signature update should be watched for a week, because the new pattern may scream or may fail open. False positives and false negatives do not replace the underlying control; they describe its error.\n\nFalse positives and false negatives documentation belongs next to each detector, with an example of each error type. False positives and false negatives for safety-critical alerts should prefer a human check over a quiet rule. False positives and false negatives metrics reported upward should include both rates so leadership does not reward a silent console. False positives and false negatives are effective when a known-patched banner case is labeled unreal without muting the check, and a deliberately unscanned host is recognized as a coverage miss.\n\nFalse positives and false negatives in a mail filter show up as a newsletter in quarantine and as a forged invoice that landed in the inbox. False positives and false negatives budgets should be set with the people who feel the pain, including the clinic staff who lose time to junk quarantines. False positives and false negatives sampling each week of closed alerts keeps the rates honest when analysts are tired. False positives and false negatives of a new rule should be shadowed, counted, and only then allowed to block.\n\nFalse positives and false negatives documentation for each rule includes one real example of noise and one real example of a catch. False positives and false negatives caused by missing inventory are coverage misses, and they should be labeled that way so nobody tunes a signature to hide an unseen subnet. False positives and false negatives after a tuning change need a before-and-after count, or the change is folklore. False positives and false negatives in application testing follow the same split: a pattern that is not exploitable, and a bug the pattern never described.\n\nFalse positives and false negatives leadership charts that show only noise will pressure teams to mute detectors. False positives and false negatives of safety alerts, such as a drug-library interaction, should tolerate more noise than a cosmetic website warning. False positives and false negatives reviews belong on the calendar of every detector owner. False positives and false negatives teaching cases should be local, drawn from the organization's own tickets, so the lesson is not abstract.\n\nFalse positives and false negatives are improved by better data, such as a credentialed scan, more often than by deleting the check.",
        'r0': 'False positives and false negatives name unreal alerts and missed real problems. False positives and false negatives include a stale banner that still looks vulnerable, and a host the scanner never authenticated to. False positives and false negatives must both be measured, because muting noise can create silence on real events. False positives and false negatives confirmation uses a second method before a finding is discarded. False positives and false negatives are properties of a detector, not a sharing association and not a market-monitoring service. False positives and false negatives proof is a labeled case with the original alert and the confirmation. False positives and false negatives reviews follow every major signature change.',
        'r1': 'False positives and false negatives are opposite detector errors. False positives and false negatives call a non-problem a problem. False positives and false negatives also miss a real problem. False positives and false negatives both need a count. False positives and false negatives worsen if rules are muted blindly. False positives and false negatives include stale-banner scan hits. False positives and false negatives include hosts the tool cannot see. False positives and false negatives need a second check. False positives and false negatives are not a sharing club. False positives and false negatives are not market monitoring. False positives and false negatives should be shown to leadership together. False positives and false negatives deserve a written tune reason.',
        'r2': "False positives and false negatives are a concert gate detector that beeps at a belt buckle and stays quiet for a hidden object. False positives and false negatives waste the guard's time on the buckle. False positives and false negatives also describe the quiet miss that matters more. False positives and false negatives get worse if the manager turns the sensitivity to zero to stop buckle beeps. False positives and false negatives are not a radio storm-net and not a pawn listing for stolen instruments. False positives and false negatives proof is a log of buckle beeps marked harmless and a test object that was caught or admitted as missed. False positives and false negatives of a brand-new detector need a week of dual checks before the old detector is removed.",
        'r4': 'False positives and false negatives: count both error types. False positives and false negatives: confirm a stale banner with a local version. False positives and false negatives: sample assets the tool cannot see. False positives and false negatives: write a reason and a review date when relaxing a rule. False positives and false negatives: avoid muting a check only to make the board green. False positives and false negatives: recheck rates after signature updates. False positives and false negatives: report both rates upward. False positives and false negatives: keep an example of each error beside the detector.',
        'exam': 'False positives and false negatives items split unreal findings from missed real ones. False positives and false negatives are not information-sharing organizations and not dark-web monitoring. False positives and false negatives tuning that only deletes alerts creates the silent error. False positives and false negatives of a banner that survived a real patch still need a second look. False positives and false negatives reporting that hides the miss rate is incomplete.',
    },
    'Prioritizing vulnerabilities': {
        'body': "Prioritizing vulnerabilities means fixing what is reachable, severe, and tied to valuable systems before spending the same energy on a long tail of low findings. Prioritizing vulnerabilities uses a public severity number as one input and then asks whether the service is exposed, whether a workaround exists, and whether active exploitation is known. Prioritizing vulnerabilities puts an internet-facing critical defect ahead of a low finding on a lab printer that cannot initiate connections. Prioritizing vulnerabilities is not the act of assigning a CVE name and is not the arithmetic of a CVSS calculator by itself.\n\nPrioritizing vulnerabilities should be a written rubric so two analysts do not invent two orders. Prioritizing vulnerabilities evidence is the ranked list with the reason, the owner, and the due date. Prioritizing vulnerabilities of a medium score on the public website can outrank a high score on a host that is powered off and unrouted. Prioritizing vulnerabilities considers compensating controls already in place, without pretending the finding disappeared.\n\nPrioritizing vulnerabilities includes asset value, so payroll and safety systems outrank a lobby display when scores are similar. Prioritizing vulnerabilities should account for whether a fix is available or only a mitigation exists. Prioritizing vulnerabilities in a change freeze still needs an emergency path for actively exploited internet-facing issues. Prioritizing vulnerabilities communication to system owners should be a short ordered list, not a raw dump.\n\nPrioritizing vulnerabilities revisits the order when a new exploit appears or when exposure changes. Prioritizing vulnerabilities does not authorize skipping the change process; it decides which change goes first. Prioritizing vulnerabilities of many findings on one host can be one maintenance window rather than twenty tickets with no plan. Prioritizing vulnerabilities metrics include aging of the top tier, not the count of low informational rows.\n\nPrioritizing vulnerabilities training stops the habit of sorting only by the vendor's color. Prioritizing vulnerabilities conflicts are resolved by the rubric and the asset owner, not by the loudest application team. Prioritizing vulnerabilities is effective when a sampled week shows the top items were reachable and important, and a low isolated finding was allowed to wait on purpose. Prioritizing vulnerabilities with a known-exploited flag should outrank a higher generic number that nobody is using in the wild, when exposure is similar.\n\nPrioritizing vulnerabilities of a safety device that cannot be patched this quarter still rises to the top of the mitigation list even if the score is modest. Prioritizing vulnerabilities across teams needs a single queue so a loud application does not consume every patch window. Prioritizing vulnerabilities of duplicate findings on one root cause should collapse into one action, such as a shared library bump. Prioritizing vulnerabilities deadlines can follow the tolerance statement: days for exposed critical items, longer for isolated lows.\n\nPrioritizing vulnerabilities of findings with no vendor fix yet should move to mitigation or acceptance rather than sitting in a fake patch queue. Prioritizing vulnerabilities input from threat intelligence, such as a campaign hitting the sector, can raise an item for a defined period. Prioritizing vulnerabilities should record why an item moved down, so the next analyst does not silently reverse it. Prioritizing vulnerabilities of test systems that hold production data are production priorities, regardless of the hostname.\n\nPrioritizing vulnerabilities meetings need the asset owner present when the top of the list is disputed. Prioritizing vulnerabilities of configuration weaknesses, such as an open share, can outrank a software flaw that is not reachable. Prioritizing vulnerabilities aging reports should turn red on the top tier before they turn red on informational noise. Prioritizing vulnerabilities of a finding that is already mitigated should show the mitigation and a lower residual rank, not a deleted row.\n\nPrioritizing vulnerabilities communication to a change board is a short ordered set, with the emergency item called out. Prioritizing vulnerabilities after a new internet exposure, such as a firewall mistake, should be recomputed the same day. Prioritizing vulnerabilities does not average ten low items into one critical item. Prioritizing vulnerabilities quality is visible when the items worked this week match the written top of the list.\n\nPrioritizing vulnerabilities of a flaw with a public exploit and a reachable path outranks a higher number that requires a local user the server does not allow. Prioritizing vulnerabilities worksheets should show the due date that follows from the tolerance band. Prioritizing vulnerabilities of an item blocked by a change freeze still carries an emergency exception path.",
        'r0': 'Prioritizing vulnerabilities orders work by reachability, severity, asset value, and known exploitation, not by the length of the report. Prioritizing vulnerabilities treats a score as an input and the local exposure as the decider. Prioritizing vulnerabilities can place a medium public flaw ahead of a higher score on an isolated powered-off host. Prioritizing vulnerabilities is not the CVE name and not the CVSS arithmetic alone. Prioritizing vulnerabilities produces an ordered list with owners and dates. Prioritizing vulnerabilities still uses the change process. Prioritizing vulnerabilities revisits order when exposure or exploitation changes. Prioritizing vulnerabilities should collapse duplicate root causes into one action. Prioritizing vulnerabilities of a test host that holds production data is a production decision. Prioritizing vulnerabilities must record why an item was moved down.',
        'r1': 'Prioritizing vulnerabilities fixes reachable pain first. Prioritizing vulnerabilities uses severity as one input. Prioritizing vulnerabilities asks who can reach the flaw. Prioritizing vulnerabilities weighs asset value. Prioritizing vulnerabilities notes known exploitation. Prioritizing vulnerabilities can rank a public medium over an isolated high. Prioritizing vulnerabilities is not a CVE name. Prioritizing vulnerabilities is not a CVSS calculator alone. Prioritizing vulnerabilities needs a written rubric. Prioritizing vulnerabilities assigns owners and dates. Prioritizing vulnerabilities still follows change control. Prioritizing vulnerabilities tracks aging of the top tier.',
        'r2': 'Prioritizing vulnerabilities is the triage nurse who sees a flood of patients and treats the person who cannot breathe before polishing a scraped knuckle. Prioritizing vulnerabilities looks at the wound and at whether the person is in the doorway or in a locked back room. Prioritizing vulnerabilities is not the case number on the chart and not the printed severity scale by itself. Prioritizing vulnerabilities writes an order the next shift can follow. Prioritizing vulnerabilities still washes hands and follows the protocol; the order is not a license to skip care. Prioritizing vulnerabilities of a loud waiting room does not outrank a quiet person in respiratory distress. Prioritizing vulnerabilities proof is a board whose first line matches the real danger, with a reason written beside it.',
        'r4': 'Prioritizing vulnerabilities: write a rubric the whole team uses. Prioritizing vulnerabilities: rank exposure and asset value with the score. Prioritizing vulnerabilities: put known-exploited internet-facing items first. Prioritizing vulnerabilities: allow isolated low items to wait on purpose. Prioritizing vulnerabilities: assign an owner and a date. Prioritizing vulnerabilities: do not sort only by vendor color. Prioritizing vulnerabilities: revisit the list when exposure changes. Prioritizing vulnerabilities: keep emergency changes inside the change path.',
        'exam': 'Prioritizing vulnerabilities items ask which flaw is fixed first, using reach and value, not only a number. Prioritizing vulnerabilities is not the CVE identifier and not the CVSS score by itself. Prioritizing vulnerabilities that always follow the highest generic number ignore the powered-off lab. Prioritizing vulnerabilities without an owner is a sorted spreadsheet, not a program. Prioritizing vulnerabilities still owes the change process a window and a backout.',
    },
    'CVE identifiers': {
        'body': "CVE identifiers give a single public handle to one known flaw so teams stop arguing from vendor nicknames. CVE identifiers look like CVE, a year, and a number, and that string is the handle to put on a ticket. CVE identifiers do not carry severity; a separate scoring system does that. CVE identifiers can apply to many products when the flawed component is shared, so the name alone does not say you are affected.\n\nCVE identifiers are assigned through numbering authorities, and a missing number does not mean the bug is imaginary, only that it may still be under a vendor advisory or embargo. CVE identifiers should be used instead of a marketing nickname when the nickname is ambiguous. CVE identifiers on a ticket let the technician open the right advisory, the right fixed version, and the right detection check. CVE identifiers are not the prioritization decision and are not the environmental score.\n\nCVE identifiers can be reserved before publication, so a blank page is not a reason to ignore a vendor warning that cites a reserved number. CVE identifiers sometimes get rejected or split, and the ticket should follow the current record rather than a stale screenshot. CVE identifiers in a software bill of materials join the component to the name automatically when versions match. CVE identifiers evidence is the full identifier string plus the vendor confirmation that this build is in range.\n\nCVE identifiers for a custom internal application may never exist, and that absence is not a clean bill of health. CVE identifiers in communication to leadership should be few and paired with plain impact, not a wall of strings. CVE identifiers help threat feeds and scanners agree, which is why the string must be copied exactly. CVE identifiers do not describe your exposure factor or your risk tolerance.\n\nCVE identifiers research notes should avoid turning a write-up into exploit steps. CVE identifiers after a fix remain on the ticket as history, marked remediated, so the name is not reused for a different bug. CVE identifiers are effective when two teams given only the string retrieve the same flaw and the same fixed version. CVE identifiers in a scanner row should be checked against the vendor advisory when the scanner's mapping looks broad.\n\nCVE identifiers can point at a library while the organization's build never calls the vulnerable function, and that reachability note belongs beside the identifier. CVE identifiers rejected by the registry should be replaced on open tickets so work is not aimed at a withdrawn number. CVE identifiers in customer conversations should be paired with the product version the customer runs. CVE identifiers for hardware firmware and for software libraries use the same kind of handle and still need different teams to act.\n\nCVE identifiers embedded in a bill of materials let a purchasing question become a maintenance question. CVE identifiers copied by hand are easy to mistype, so paste from the advisory and then read the year and the digits back. CVE identifiers do not tell you the exploit difficulty; the vector and the local exposure do that work. CVE identifiers in a change ticket should remain stable even if the title text is edited for clarity.\n\nCVE identifiers of a flaw that spans a protocol implementation may appear on many appliances, and each appliance owner still confirms applicability. CVE identifiers under embargo may be known to a vendor contact before the public page exists, and the ticket can cite the vendor bulletin until the handle is public. CVE identifiers in detection content should match the scanner and the endpoint tool so three consoles do not use three nicknames. CVE identifiers training for new analysts is mostly discipline: copy the handle, confirm the version, write the local note.\n\nCVE identifiers of a disputed bug, where the vendor disagrees the issue is theirs, should record both the handle and the dispute. CVE identifiers history on a remediated host helps a later audit show the flaw was once present and then cleared. CVE identifiers are a coordination tool across vendors, scanners, and people, which is their entire job. CVE identifiers in a status report should be few, each paired with the local asset count rather than printed as a bare wall of strings.\n\nCVE identifiers that alias one another in a vendor matrix should be noted so two tickets are not closed as if they were unrelated.",
        'r0': 'CVE identifiers give one public string to one known vulnerability so teams do not argue from nicknames. CVE identifiers are names, not severity scores and not a local priority. CVE identifiers may cover a shared component across products, so applicability is a separate check. CVE identifiers can be missing while a real vendor advisory exists. CVE identifiers belong verbatim on the ticket with the affected version range. CVE identifiers are not CVSS, not the prioritization rubric, and not exposure factor. CVE identifiers proof is two people reaching the same flaw from the same string. CVE identifiers copied by hand should be read back digit by digit. CVE identifiers that the registry rejects need to be replaced on open tickets. CVE identifiers under embargo can be tracked from the vendor bulletin until the public page exists.',
        'r1': 'CVE identifiers name one public flaw. CVE identifiers include a year and a number. CVE identifiers are not a severity score. CVE identifiers are not CVSS. CVE identifiers are not your local priority. CVE identifiers can cover many products. CVE identifiers do not prove you are affected. CVE identifiers can be missing for new bugs. CVE identifiers should be copied exactly. CVE identifiers beat ambiguous nicknames. CVE identifiers stay on the ticket after the fix. CVE identifiers are not exposure or tolerance.',
        'r2': 'CVE identifiers are the catalog number on a library book so two librarians fetch the same edition. CVE identifiers do not say whether the book is urgent to read. CVE identifiers do not say the branch on your street owns a copy. CVE identifiers are not the stamp that ranks how stormy the week is and not the decision of which shelf to clear first. CVE identifiers of a nickname like the red book fail when the library owns three red books. CVE identifiers that were withdrawn should be updated so the card catalog does not point at a blank. CVE identifiers proof is two desks producing the same volume from the same number.',
        'r4': 'CVE identifiers: copy the full string onto the ticket. CVE identifiers: confirm the version range applies here. CVE identifiers: do not treat the name as a severity. CVE identifiers: keep using a vendor advisory if no number exists yet. CVE identifiers: follow splits and rejections in the public record. CVE identifiers: pair the string with a plain impact for leadership. CVE identifiers: leave the name in history after remediation. CVE identifiers: separate this label from CVSS and from priority.',
        'exam': 'CVE identifiers items ask for the shared public name of a flaw, not its score. CVE identifiers are not CVSS and are not the act of prioritizing work. CVE identifiers without an applicability check can send a team fixing a product they do not run. CVE identifiers that are nicknames instead of the canonical string cause mismatched tickets. CVE identifiers can be absent while the bug is still real.',
    },
    'CVSS scores': {
        'body': 'CVSS scores are standardized severity numbers for a vulnerability, usually from zero to ten, derived from a vector of attack path, complexity, privileges, user interaction, scope, and effects on confidentiality, integrity, and availability. CVSS scores come in a base vector that ignores your environment, plus temporal and environmental adjustments when those are used. CVSS scores open the severity discussion, and local reach still decides whether that number waits or moves. CVSS scores may look severe on an unplugged lab host and still lose the week to a milder flaw on the internet-facing site.\n\nCVSS scores are not the CVE name; the name identifies the flaw and the score rates a severity. CVSS scores are not the full prioritization rubric, which also weighs asset value and active exploitation. CVSS scores should be cited with the version of the formula and the vector string, because a bare 9.8 cannot be audited. CVSS scores from a vendor and from a national database sometimes differ when the assumptions differ, and the ticket should say which vector was used.\n\nCVSS scores environmental metrics let you lower or raise the number when confidentiality requirements or modified attack paths differ locally. CVSS scores do not express exposure factor as a fraction of asset loss and do not express risk tolerance. CVSS scores of zero or near zero can still matter if the finding is a policy violation the rubric treats separately. CVSS scores should not be averaged across a host into a meaningless mean.\n\nCVSS scores communication to leadership works better as bands plus the top items than as a sea of decimals. CVSS scores evidence is the vector, the resulting number, and the source. CVSS scores after a mitigation may be recomputed with environmental metrics, with the original base left visible. CVSS scores training stops analysts from treating the color as the only sort key.\n\nCVSS scores are effective when two analysts given the same vector produce the same base number and then still write a local exposure note that can change the order. CVSS scores base vectors describe the worst reasonable case the publisher assumed, which may not be the configuration in this building. CVSS scores temporal values can fall when a fix exists and rise when exploit code is known, and those shifts should be dated. CVSS scores environmental modifications for a flaw that requires a local account can drop when that path is unavailable on the server.\n\nCVSS scores should be stored as the vector, not only the decimal, so a later formula change can be recomputed. CVSS scores disagreements between a vendor and a database are resolved by writing which one the rubric will use this quarter. CVSS scores of a flaw with a low confidentiality impact and a high availability impact matter more on a safety controller than on a brochure site. CVSS scores do not encode whether a control already blocks the attack path; the environmental or the local note does.\n\nCVSS scores in a dashboard can be grouped into bands for executives while analysts keep the vector. CVSS scores training should include one exercise where the same base number produces two different work orders because exposure differs. CVSS scores of chained bugs are not found by adding the decimals together. CVSS scores used as a contractual service level, such as a promise to fix above a band within a number of days, should match the tolerance statement.\n\nCVSS scores on a withdrawn issue should be removed from the active queue with the identifier. CVSS scores flavor, such as version three versus a newer revision, must be labeled because the numbers are not perfectly comparable. CVSS scores of a configuration finding may be absent, and the rubric still needs a way to rank that finding. CVSS scores are useful because they are shared language, and they are dangerous when they replace local judgment.\n\nCVSS scores in an audit sample should be reproducible by a second person from the stored vector. CVSS scores used in a contract should name the formula revision so a later revision does not silently rewrite the obligation. CVSS scores for a flaw that needs user interaction can be read differently on a kiosk than on an administrator workstation. CVSS scores stored without the vector should be treated as incomplete records.\n\nCVSS scores are a shared scale, and the local note is what makes the scale operational.',
        'r0': 'CVSS scores turn a vector of exploitability and impact into a severity number, typically zero through ten. CVSS scores include a base view and optional temporal and environmental adjustments. CVSS scores start the discussion and do not finish it, because local exposure can reorder work. CVSS scores are not CVE identifiers, which only name the flaw. CVSS scores should travel with the vector string and the formula version. CVSS scores must not be averaged into a single host grade. CVSS scores differ from exposure factor and from risk tolerance. CVSS scores proof is a reproducible vector plus a written local note. CVSS scores should be stored as the vector so they can be recomputed. CVSS scores from two publishers need a written choice when they disagree. CVSS scores of chained bugs are not a sum of decimals.',
        'r1': 'CVSS scores are standardized severity numbers. CVSS scores usually run from zero to ten. CVSS scores come from a vector string. CVSS scores have a base view. CVSS scores can add environmental adjustments. CVSS scores start the conversation. CVSS scores do not replace local exposure. CVSS scores are not CVE names. CVSS scores are not the full priority rubric. CVSS scores should not be averaged away. CVSS scores are not exposure factor. CVSS scores are not risk tolerance. CVSS scores should keep the vector string. CVSS scores from two sources need a written choice.',
        'r2': "CVSS scores are the avalanche number posted on a chalkboard at the trailhead. CVSS scores tell you the general danger the bulletin writers saw. CVSS scores do not walk your particular slope, which may be sheltered or may sit under a cornice. CVSS scores are not the catalog number of a single book and not the nurse's order of patients. CVSS scores of a high bulletin on a closed trail can wait while a moderate bulletin on the open ridge gets attention. CVSS scores should be copied with the symbols that produced them, not as a lonely digit. CVSS scores proof is the same number from the same symbols, plus a note about your own ridge.",
        'r4': "CVSS scores: record the vector and the formula version. CVSS scores: keep the base number visible. CVSS scores: add an environmental note when local exposure differs. CVSS scores: do not sort solely by the band. CVSS scores: do not average scores across a host. CVSS scores: say which publisher's vector you used when they differ. CVSS scores: separate the number from the CVE name. CVSS scores: separate the number from priority, exposure factor, and tolerance.",
        'exam': "CVSS scores items are severity numbers from a published vector, not the flaw's name. CVSS scores are not CVE identifiers and are not the whole prioritization decision. CVSS scores without the vector cannot be checked. CVSS scores that ignore a powered-off host will mis-order the week. CVSS scores are a start, and the environment finishes the argument.",
    },
    'Exposure factor': {
        'body': "Exposure factor estimates how large a slice of an asset a named event would destroy, often written as a percentage or a fraction. Exposure factor answers how much of the thing is gone, not how often the event happens. Exposure factor for an unencrypted laptop theft can be the whole data set on that disk, so the fraction is total for that asset. Exposure factor for a redundant array with a quick rebuild may be only the cost of the rebuild and a short outage, not the entire service value.\n\nExposure factor feeds single loss expectancy when it is multiplied by asset value, and that product is still not the annual figure until frequency enters. Exposure factor is not environmental and business impact in the broad sense, though they are neighbors: one is a fraction of value, the other is the concrete harm to process, law, and customers. Exposure factor is not risk tolerance, which is how much risk leadership will carry. Exposure factor should be estimated per scenario, because the same server has a different fraction for theft, for ransomware, and for a brief outage.\n\nExposure factor evidence is the scenario, the asset, the fraction, and the assumption behind it. Exposure factor workshops with finance stop security staff from inventing asset values alone. Exposure factor of a backup that is intact lowers the fraction for some events and does nothing for a confidentiality event if the stolen copy was plaintext. Exposure factor documentation should show the fraction as a reasoned range when precision is fake.\n\nExposure factor changes when encryption or segmentation is added, and the record should be updated. Exposure factor should not be confused with a public flaw handle or with a severity vector. Exposure factor misuse happens when people treat it as a probability. Exposure factor is effective when two scenarios for one asset carry different fractions and the higher fraction matches the event that actually destroys more of the asset.\n\nExposure factor workshops should use a handful of scenarios the organization actually fears, not a generic catalog copied from a textbook. Exposure factor for a confidentiality event is often higher than for an availability event on the same disk, because a backup restores service and does not un-steal a copy. Exposure factor ranges, such as a low and a high fraction, are more honest than a fake-precise decimal when the architecture is poorly understood. Exposure factor assumptions should name whether encryption, backups, and segmentation were already counted.\n\nExposure factor of a shared database is not the fraction of one table if the credential that leaks opens every table. Exposure factor updates after a major architecture change belong on the same ticket as the change. Exposure factor for a ransomware scenario includes the fraction of systems that would be rebuilt, which may be most of the estate if flat networks allow spread. Exposure factor teaching should warn analysts not to multiply the fraction by likelihood inside the same cell.\n\nExposure factor evidence for finance is the fraction, the asset value they agreed, and the scenario name. Exposure factor of a paper process and of a digital process can differ for the same business service. Exposure factor arguments that cannot be settled should be recorded as a range and still used, rather than left blank. Exposure factor of a vendor-hosted service may be the fraction of the business process lost during an outage, which is an organization question more than a server question.\n\nExposure factor quality shows when a later incident's real loss lands inside the range that was written. Exposure factor is a planning input to loss estimates, and it stays silent about whether leadership is willing to carry that loss. Exposure factor of a credential leak includes every system that trusts that credential, not only the vault row where it was stored. Exposure factor discussions should separate rebuild cost from notification cost when both apply to one scenario.\n\nExposure factor of an intact offline backup lowers availability loss and does not lower confidentiality loss. Exposure factor records should cite the architecture diagram version they assumed. Exposure factor disagreements between two analysts are resolved by writing the range both can defend. Exposure factor remains silent about whether the organization is willing to carry the resulting loss.\n\nExposure factor worksheets should show the slice as a percent and the scenario name side by side.",
        'r0': 'Exposure factor is the fraction of an asset lost if a named event happens. Exposure factor is not the likelihood of that event. Exposure factor can be total for plaintext data on a stolen laptop and partial for a short outage of a redundant service. Exposure factor times asset value estimates a single loss, before frequency makes it annual. Exposure factor is distinct from business-impact narrative and from risk tolerance. Exposure factor is per scenario, not a permanent tattoo on the asset. Exposure factor proof is a fraction with an assumption, updated when controls change. Exposure factor for theft of a copy often exceeds the fraction for a short outage, because backups restore service only. Exposure factor assumptions must say whether encryption was already counted. Exposure factor should stay a range when false precision would mislead.',
        'r1': 'Exposure factor is the portion lost if an event hits. Exposure factor is a fraction of the asset. Exposure factor is not how often the event happens. Exposure factor can be total for a plaintext theft. Exposure factor can be small for a short redundant outage. Exposure factor feeds a single-loss estimate. Exposure factor is not the annual rate. Exposure factor is not business-impact narrative alone. Exposure factor is not risk tolerance. Exposure factor is not a CVE or a CVSS number. Exposure factor changes when controls change. Exposure factor must name the scenario.',
        'r2': 'Exposure factor is the flood line drawn across a painting, showing how much of the canvas would be ruined if that flood happens. Exposure factor of a line at the top means the whole picture goes. Exposure factor of a line at the hem means a restorable edge. Exposure factor does not say whether the river will rise this year. Exposure factor is not the story of Saturday customers turned away and not the rule for how much risk the gallery will accept. Exposure factor changes if the painting is hung higher. Exposure factor proof is a mark on one painting for one kind of flood, with a note about why the mark sits there.',
        'r4': 'Exposure factor: name the scenario before choosing a fraction. Exposure factor: do not use the fraction as a probability. Exposure factor: set a total fraction for unprotected data theft when that is honest. Exposure factor: lower the fraction only with a control that truly limits loss. Exposure factor: multiply by asset value only when finance agrees on value. Exposure factor: keep frequency as a separate input. Exposure factor: update the fraction after encryption or segmentation. Exposure factor: separate this number from tolerance and from impact stories.',
        'exam': 'Exposure factor items ask what fraction of the asset a named event destroys. Exposure factor is not likelihood, not risk tolerance, and not the full business-impact story. Exposure factor for plaintext theft of a laptop can be the whole data set. Exposure factor for a brief outage of a redundant service can be small. Exposure factor that never changes after a new control was not tied to reality.',
    },
    'Environmental and business impact': {
        'body': "Environmental and business impact ask what a flaw or an event does in this organization: which process stops, which obligation applies, and which customer or patient is harmed. Environmental and business impact of the same bug on a lobby kiosk and on the payroll server are not equal, even when a generic score matches. Environmental and business impact includes downtime, safety, contractual penalties, and reputation, stated in local terms rather than in a template. Environmental and business impact is not the exposure-factor fraction, though the fraction may inform the size of the loss.\n\nEnvironmental and business impact is not risk tolerance, which is the appetite boundary leadership sets. Environmental and business impact should be written by the process owner with security, not by a scanner. Environmental and business impact evidence is a short statement tied to a system and a scenario. Environmental and business impact of a public website defacement differs from a silent integrity change in a clinical record.\n\nEnvironmental and business impact reviews belong in prioritization meetings so the rubric stays honest. Environmental and business impact of a vendor outage includes whether a manual procedure exists. Environmental and business impact changes when the system moves from a pilot to full production. Environmental and business impact language for executives avoids raw check identifiers and names the process.\n\nEnvironmental and business impact is not a CVE string and not a CVSS vector. Environmental and business impact of data exposure includes who the data is about and what notice duties wake up. Environmental and business impact estimates can be qualitative bands when dollars would be fiction, and quantitative when finance has real figures. Environmental and business impact is effective when two systems with the same technical bug receive different impact statements and the higher one matches the process the organization cannot pause.\n\nEnvironmental and business impact statements should name the hours a process can pause before harm becomes serious, which is different for a payroll cutoff than for a brochure site. Environmental and business impact of a confidentiality failure includes who must be told and how fast, in the organization's own duty language. Environmental and business impact of an integrity failure in a clinical record is not the same shape as an integrity failure in a cafeteria menu. Environmental and business impact interviews with the process owner should be short and concrete: what stops, who calls, what fine or harm follows.\n\nEnvironmental and business impact of a peak-season outage can exceed the impact of the same outage on a quiet day, so the note can mention timing. Environmental and business impact is an input to priority and to tolerance discussions, and it is rewritten when the business changes. Environmental and business impact of a supplier failure includes whether patients or customers can be served manually. Environmental and business impact scales can be simple bands, such as limited, serious, and severe, if the words are defined with examples.\n\nEnvironmental and business impact of a test system should say whether real personal data is present, because that fact dominates the technical bug. Environmental and business impact writing should avoid copying a generic score rationale in place of a local sentence. Environmental and business impact of safety equipment includes physical harm, which outranks inconvenience even when the revenue number is small. Environmental and business impact reviews after an incident should compare the predicted harm with what actually happened and adjust the note.\n\nEnvironmental and business impact owners sign the sentence, so a later argument has a name attached. Environmental and business impact of the same flaw in two regions may differ when the legal duty differs. Environmental and business impact is the local story of harm, told specifically enough that two reasonable people would not swap the kiosk and the system of record. Environmental and business impact of a missed payroll file includes employees, regulators, and the manual workaround, each in one sentence.\n\nEnvironmental and business impact of a kiosk outage includes embarrassment and lost transactions, not clinical harm, unless the kiosk is actually clinical. Environmental and business impact notes should be updated when a manual workaround is retired. Environmental and business impact of a multi-region service should say whether one region can carry the load. Environmental and business impact language should be specific enough that a new manager can retell it without the scanner open.\n\nEnvironmental and business impact of a public notice includes who drafts it and how fast it must leave.",
        'r0': "Environmental and business impact describes local harm: process stoppage, legal duty, safety, and customer effect. Environmental and business impact refuses to treat a kiosk and a payroll host as the same outcome. Environmental and business impact is written with the process owner. Environmental and business impact describes local harm and should not be stored in the loss-fraction field or in the leadership-boundary field. Environmental and business impact can be qualitative or tied to real figures. Environmental and business impact feeds prioritization and executive language. Environmental and business impact proof is a scenario statement that would change if the system moved from pilot to production. Environmental and business impact should state how long a process can pause before harm spikes. Environmental and business impact of safety harm outranks a small revenue figure. Environmental and business impact notes need the process owner's name.",
        'r1': 'Environmental and business impact asks what happens here. Environmental and business impact names the process that stops. Environmental and business impact names who is harmed. Environmental and business impact can include legal duties. Environmental and business impact differs for the same bug on two systems. Environmental and business impact is not exposure factor. Environmental and business impact is not risk tolerance. Environmental and business impact is not a CVE string. Environmental and business impact is not a CVSS vector. Environmental and business impact needs the process owner. Environmental and business impact can be a qualitative band. Environmental and business impact should change when the system becomes critical.',
        'r2': 'Environmental and business impact is the difference between a cancelled ferry route and a closed museum cafe on the same stormy day. Environmental and business impact of the ferry is stranded commuters and a contract fine. Environmental and business impact of the cafe is a quieter loss of pastry sales. Environmental and business impact is not the flood line on one painting and not the rule for how much spice the kitchen will serve. Environmental and business impact is told by the ferry master and the cafe lead, not by a weather robot alone. Environmental and business impact changes if the cafe becomes the only kitchen for a hospital ward. Environmental and business impact proof is two different harm notes for one storm.',
        'r4': "Environmental and business impact: write the harmed process in local words. Environmental and business impact: involve the process owner. Environmental and business impact: separate a kiosk from a system of record. Environmental and business impact: note notice duties when data is exposed. Environmental and business impact: include manual workarounds if they exist. Environmental and business impact: update the note when the system becomes production-critical. Environmental and business impact: keep the fraction of asset loss as a different field. Environmental and business impact: keep leadership's tolerance boundary as a different field.",
        'exam': 'Environmental and business impact items describe local harm, not a generic score. Environmental and business impact is not exposure factor and not risk tolerance. Environmental and business impact of one bug on two systems can differ. Environmental and business impact without a process owner is a guess. Environmental and business impact should change the order of work when the harmed process is critical.',
    },
    'Risk tolerance': {
        'body': "Risk tolerance is the boundary leadership sets for residual risk, and operators use it to tell a waiting item from an item that must move. Risk tolerance is set by leadership in words or numbers the operators can apply, not by each analyst's mood. Risk tolerance can accept a low finding on a lab for a defined period and refuse an internet-facing critical defect overnight. Risk tolerance draws on loss size and on local harm without becoming either of those notes.\n\nRisk tolerance should be visible in the exception process so a silent shrug cannot substitute for an accepted risk. Risk tolerance may differ by business line when a hospital clinical system and a marketing site do not share the same boundary. Risk tolerance evidence is the written boundary, the person who approved it, and the date it was reviewed. Risk tolerance that is never tested against real findings is a poster.\n\nRisk tolerance communication tells system owners what they may accept locally and what must be escalated. Risk tolerance reviews follow major incidents and at least annually, because yesterday's boundary may be too loose or too tight. Risk tolerance should not be mistaken for a catalog string or for a base severity. Risk tolerance does not mean ignoring unknowns; it means deciding with the information in hand and naming what is still uncertain.\n\nRisk tolerance for safety issues may be near zero even when financial tolerance is broader. Risk tolerance documentation should include examples, such as the lab finding that may wait and the public critical item that may not. Risk tolerance conflicts between a delivery deadline and the boundary go upward, not into a quiet waiver. Risk tolerance is effective when a sample of open exceptions all sit inside the written boundary and anything outside has an escalation.\n\nRisk tolerance statements should be specific enough that an analyst can classify a finding without calling a meeting every time. Risk tolerance examples belong in the same document as the boundary, including one item that waits and one that does not. Risk tolerance for regulated data may be tighter than for public marketing content, and both lines should be visible. Risk tolerance decisions are leadership decisions, and security staff prepare the options rather than silently choosing.\n\nRisk tolerance that conflicts with a delivery date is escalated with the impact note attached, not bargained away in a hallway. Risk tolerance reviews after a near miss should ask whether the boundary was wrong or the boundary was ignored. Risk tolerance for third parties can require a higher bar than for an internal lab, because the organization cannot patch the third party directly. Risk tolerance language should avoid vague words like reasonable unless an example pins them down.\n\nRisk tolerance and the exception tracker should use the same categories so an accept can be checked against the line. Risk tolerance of safety findings can be described as escalate always, which is a valid and strict boundary. Risk tolerance communication to new managers should be part of onboarding, or they will invent a looser line under schedule pressure. Risk tolerance changes should be versioned, and open exceptions should be re-read against the new version.\n\nRisk tolerance is tested by sampling, not by the existence of a poster on an intranet page. Risk tolerance does not require every risk to be eliminated, and it does require the remainder to be named. Risk tolerance of an unknown, such as an un-scanned subnet, is not automatically inside the boundary; unknowns are escalated until they are measured. Risk tolerance for a marketing site can allow a longer wait than a medication cabinet controller, and both examples should be written.\n\nRisk tolerance statements that say only low risk is too vague for an analyst on a Friday afternoon. Risk tolerance sampling each quarter should pick five open accepts and see whether each one still matches the written line. Risk tolerance breaches, meaning an item carried outside the line with no escalation, are themselves findings. Risk tolerance of a temporary pilot can be looser only when the pilot holds no real personal data and has an end date.\n\nRisk tolerance documents should name the leadership role that owns the boundary. Risk tolerance does not shrink because a project is late, unless that role explicitly redraws the line. Risk tolerance posters without the examples are decoration, not a boundary an analyst can apply.",
        'r0': 'Risk tolerance is the leadership boundary for how much risk may remain. Risk tolerance tells operators which findings can wait and which must move now. Risk tolerance is written, owned, and dated, and it is applied through the exception path. Risk tolerance can be stricter for safety than for a marketing site. Risk tolerance is not exposure factor and not the business-impact story, though it uses both. Risk tolerance is not a severity score. Risk tolerance proof is a set of open accepts that fit the boundary and escalations for anything that does not. Risk tolerance examples should sit in the same document as the boundary. Risk tolerance for an unscanned subnet is not automatically acceptable. Risk tolerance changes should force a reread of open exceptions.',
        'r1': 'Risk tolerance is how much risk leadership will carry. Risk tolerance decides what may wait. Risk tolerance decides what may not wait. Risk tolerance must be written down. Risk tolerance needs an owner and a review date. Risk tolerance can differ by business line. Risk tolerance is stricter for safety issues. Risk tolerance is not exposure factor. Risk tolerance is not business-impact text alone. Risk tolerance is not a CVSS number. Risk tolerance is applied through exceptions. Risk tolerance is checked against real open items.',
        'r2': "Risk tolerance is the climbing gym's rule for which routes stay open when the holds are worn. Risk tolerance leaves an easy practice wall up with a small chip and closes the overhang above the concrete the same day. Risk tolerance is posted where setters can read it, not invented by whoever closes up. Risk tolerance is not the flood line on a painting and not the story of a cancelled ferry. Risk tolerance of a kids' wall is tighter than the tolerance of a members' wall. Risk tolerance proof is a chip that matches the posted rule and an overhang that is roped off because it does not. Risk tolerance fails when a setter leaves the overhang open because a competition is tomorrow.",
        'r4': 'Risk tolerance: write the boundary with leadership. Risk tolerance: give examples of what may wait and what may not. Risk tolerance: name who approves and when it is reviewed. Risk tolerance: allow different boundaries per business line. Risk tolerance: keep safety tighter than convenience. Risk tolerance: force exceptions through that boundary. Risk tolerance: escalate conflicts instead of quiet waivers. Risk tolerance: sample open accepts against the written line.',
        'exam': 'Risk tolerance items ask how much risk the organization will carry, as a written boundary. Risk tolerance is not exposure factor and not environmental and business impact. Risk tolerance is not a CVSS score. Risk tolerance that lives only in habit will disagree with itself by Friday. Risk tolerance shows up when a lab item waits and an internet-facing critical item does not.',
    },
    'Patch remediation': {
        'body': "Patch remediation is the change that puts a fix onto systems, with a test, a pilot, a schedule, and a way back. Patch remediation is not the scan that found the flaw and not the report that told leadership about it. Patch remediation starts once prioritization has said this fix is the one to land now. Patch remediation in a pilot group comes before the rest of the fleet, and the pilot must be large enough to see real breakage.\n\nPatch remediation backout is rehearsed or at least written, including the previous package version and the person who may invoke it. Patch remediation windows respect clinical or business blackouts unless the rubric called the item emergency. Patch remediation evidence is the change record, the versions before and after, and the pilot result. Patch remediation of an application library is a build and a deploy, not only a host reboot.\n\nPatch remediation fails when the package is installed and the vulnerable feature is still loaded from another path. Patch remediation coordination includes owners of the system, not only the patch team. Patch remediation metrics include time from decision to completed pilot, not how many files were downloaded. Patch remediation is distinct from a compensating barrier used because the fix cannot land yet.\n\nPatch remediation is distinct from a written exception that accepts the finding for a while. Patch remediation is distinct from the rescan that proves the finding left, though the rescan follows it. Patch remediation is distinct from the executive summary. Patch remediation is effective when the pilot stays healthy and the following rings match the piloted version.\n\nPatch remediation rings can follow rings of the fleet, such as a canary site, then a region, then the rest, with health checks between rings. Patch remediation of a database cluster should state whether the fix is applied on a secondary first. Patch remediation packages should be retrieved from a trusted internal mirror so a compromised vendor CDN is not the only source. Patch remediation success criteria include the service health check, not only the package manager's success code.\n\nPatch remediation of container images is a rebuild and a rollout, and the old image should be blocked from new scheduling. Patch remediation tickets that span hundreds of hosts can still name the ring and the failure threshold that stops the ring. Patch remediation communication to a service desk should say what users might see, so a reboot is not mistaken for an outage caused by an attacker. Patch remediation of emergency items still records who approved the shortened test.\n\nPatch remediation dependencies, such as a library that requires a runtime bump, should be visible before the window. Patch remediation of network gear includes a rollback image on the device, not only a hope that the vendor file can be downloaded again during the failure.",
        'r0': 'Patch remediation applies the fix through change control, with a pilot and a backout. Patch remediation records versions before and after. Patch remediation is not the discovery scan, the priority meeting, the fence used while waiting, the accepted exception, the verification rescan, or the leadership summary. Patch remediation of libraries means a new build, not a reboot slogan. Patch remediation proof is a healthy pilot followed by the same version on the next ring. Patch remediation emergency path still names a backout. Patch remediation rings should stop when a health check fails. Patch remediation images ought to come from an internal mirror. Patch remediation of containers means a new image is the only one scheduling is allowed to run. Patch remediation success is a health check, not only an installer code.',
        'r1': 'Patch remediation puts the fix on systems. Patch remediation uses the change process. Patch remediation starts with a pilot. Patch remediation needs a written backout. Patch remediation records old and new versions. Patch remediation respects blackout windows. Patch remediation is not the scan. Patch remediation is not a temporary fence. Patch remediation is not an accepted exception. Patch remediation is not the rescan. Patch remediation is not the executive note. Patch remediation checks that the new build is the one actually running.',
        'r2': 'Patch remediation is replacing a cracked violin string with a spare from the case, after trying the spare on one instrument. Patch remediation keeps the old string in an envelope until the new one holds tune through rehearsal. Patch remediation is not noticing the crack, not roping off the chair, not a signed note to delay, not listening again after the swap, and not the note to the conductor. Patch remediation of the whole section happens only after the first violin stays true. Patch remediation proof is the same spare brand on the next stand, with the old string still in the envelope.',
        'r4': 'Patch remediation: pilot before the full fleet. Patch remediation: write the backout and the prior version. Patch remediation: record the change window. Patch remediation: confirm the running version, not only the installer log. Patch remediation: include the system owner. Patch remediation: keep emergency work inside a change record. Patch remediation: do not call a firewall rule the patch. Patch remediation: hand the result to verification afterward.',
        'exam': 'Patch remediation items are the fix landing through change control with a way back. Patch remediation is not a compensating control, an exception, a rescan, or the leadership report. Patch remediation without a pilot is the reckless distractor. Patch remediation that installs a package but leaves the old library loaded is unfinished. Patch remediation still owes verification a before-and-after version.',
    },
    'Compensating controls for vulnerabilities': {
        'body': 'Compensating controls for vulnerabilities reduce risk when the patch cannot land yet, by fencing exposure or adding a detective layer. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities include allowing a service to answer just the administration jump host, disabling the vulnerable feature, or adding a targeted filter in front of the application. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities do not erase the finding; they change the exposure until remediation or acceptance is recorded. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities must be specific, because a slogan to monitor the system is not a control.\n\nCompensating controls for vulnerabilities evidence is the rule or configuration, the test that an unauthorized path fails, and the finding identifier it covers. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities are temporary when a patch is expected, and the expiry should be visible. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities are not the patch itself. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities are not the signed exception, though an exception often depends on them.\n\nCompensating controls for vulnerabilities are not the rescan, which may show the original flaw still present while the fence holds. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities are not the monthly leadership note. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities of an unpatchable instrument can be network isolation plus a jump host and extra logging. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities should be reviewed when the network changes, or a new route walks around the fence.\n\nCompensating controls for vulnerabilities ownership sits with the team who can break the fence by a casual firewall edit. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities are effective when a test from outside the allowed path fails and a test from the allowed path still meets the business need. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities should name the attack path they interrupt, such as remote code execution from the internet, so a control aimed at a different path is not mistaken for coverage. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities that rely on a person watching an alert need an on-call rotation or they are not controls at night.\n\nCompensating controls for vulnerabilities implemented as a firewall rule should include the rule identifier in the finding record. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities for an appliance that cannot be patched may combine isolation, a jump host, and a vendor-supported configuration hardening. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities must be tested from the real untrusted network, not only from a diagram. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities that disable a feature should confirm the business process has another way to work.\n\nCompensating controls for vulnerabilities drift is a scheduled check, because a later project will reopen a port or a route for convenience. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities do not lower the CVE out of existence; they lower the residual exposure the rubric uses. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities in front of a web flaw, such as a strict filter, should be paired with logging of blocked payloads. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities ownership includes the team that owns the firewall or the feature flag, not only the team that owns the vulnerable host.',
        'r0': 'Compensating controls for vulnerabilities shrink exposure while a patch is delayed or impossible. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities are concrete configurations, such as a source restriction, a disabled feature, or a filter, plus a test. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities leave the underlying flaw in place. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities need an owner and a review when paths change. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities are not the patch, the waiver, the rescan, or the executive report, though they often support a waiver. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities proof is a denied unauthorized path and a working authorized path. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities must name the path they interrupt. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities that depend on a human need an on-call rotation. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities are tested from the untrusted side, not from a diagram.',
        'r1': 'Compensating controls for vulnerabilities reduce risk without the patch. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities can limit who may connect. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities can disable the weak feature. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities leave the flaw in place. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities need a real configuration. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities need a test. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities are not the patch. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities are not the waiver text. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities are not the rescan. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities expire or get reviewed. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities break if a new path appears. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities must still allow the business path.',
        'r2': 'Compensating controls for vulnerabilities are a velvet rope around a wet gallery floor until the tile can be replaced. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities do not dry the tile. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities fail if a side door still opens onto the wet stone. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities are not the new tile, not the permit to leave the floor wet all year, not a second look at the puddle, and not the letter to the board. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities include a guard who actually stops a visitor, not a rope lying on the ground. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities proof is a visitor turned back and a curator who can still cross by the allowed plank.',
        'r4': 'Compensating controls for vulnerabilities: tie each fence to a finding identifier. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities: test the blocked path and the allowed path. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities: name an owner. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities: date the review. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities: recheck after firewall or route changes. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities: do not describe the fence as a completed patch. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities: pair it with an exception if the flaw will remain. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities: prefer a specific rule over a vague monitoring promise.',
        'exam': 'Compensating controls for vulnerabilities items reduce exposure while the flaw remains. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities are not patch remediation, not the exception document, not rescanning, and not the status report. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities that nobody tests are imaginary. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities must fail closed for the unauthorized path. Compensating controls for vulnerabilities still need a later verify or a recorded acceptance.',
    },
    'Exceptions and exemptions': {
        'body': 'Exceptions and exemptions are written decisions to live with a finding, and silence is neither. Exceptions and exemptions use the word exception for a time-boxed acceptance with an owner, a reason, and usually a compensating control. Exceptions and exemptions use the word exemption for a formal waiver when the standard will not be met and leadership accepts that openly. Exceptions and exemptions both expire or get reviewed; an undated note is a shrug.\n\nExceptions and exemptions evidence is the signed record, the finding identifier, the end date, and the residual risk statement. Exceptions and exemptions are not the patch that removes the finding. Exceptions and exemptions are not the fence by itself, though the fence often justifies the exception. Exceptions and exemptions are not the rescan and not the leadership digest, though both may mention them.\n\nExceptions and exemptions for a legacy application should name the business process that blocks the patch. Exceptions and exemptions routes should be harder for internet-facing critical items than for an isolated lab, matching risk tolerance. Exceptions and exemptions that outlive their date must be renewed or closed, and the tracker should surface them without a human remembering. Exceptions and exemptions ownership cannot be a shared mailbox with no person.\n\nExceptions and exemptions communication tells auditors the truth: the organization knows, and here is the boundary. Exceptions and exemptions are effective when every open item past its review date is either renewed in writing or back on the remediation list. Exceptions and exemptions forms should include the finding identifier, the asset, the reason a fix is deferred, the residual risk, the compensating control if any, the owner, and the review date. Exceptions and exemptions for a critical exposed service should require a higher approver than an isolated low finding.\n\nExceptions and exemptions language should be plain enough that the owner understands the harm they are accepting. Exceptions and exemptions cannot be granted by the same person who will not feel the incident, when the tolerance statement says otherwise. Exceptions and exemptions trackers should show age and should page the owner before the date, not after. Exceptions and exemptions bundled across unrelated findings hide risk and should be split.\n\nExceptions and exemptions of a permanent waiver still need a periodic confirmation that the business reason remains true. Exceptions and exemptions denied by leadership should return to the remediation queue with a due date. Exceptions and exemptions stored only in email are not a system of record. Exceptions and exemptions metrics include how many expired, how many were renewed, and how many were closed by a real fix.\n\nExceptions and exemptions for a vendor-imposed limitation should attach the vendor statement so the reason can be checked later.',
        'r0': 'Exceptions and exemptions record a conscious choice to carry a finding for a period or under a formal waiver. Exceptions and exemptions require an owner, a reason, an end or review date, and a residual-risk note. Exceptions and exemptions are not silence and not a backlog comment. Exceptions and exemptions often rely on a compensating control but are not that control. Exceptions and exemptions are not the patch, the rescan, or the monthly report. Exceptions and exemptions must match the tolerance boundary, with a higher bar for exposed critical flaws. Exceptions and exemptions proof is a dated record that cannot hide past its review. Exceptions and exemptions should page the owner before the review date. Exceptions and exemptions stored only in mail are not a system of record.',
        'r1': 'Exceptions and exemptions are written acceptances. Exceptions and exemptions give an exception a time box. Exceptions and exemptions give an exemption a formal waiver. Exceptions and exemptions always name an owner. Exceptions and exemptions always include a date. Exceptions and exemptions are not silence. Exceptions and exemptions are not the patch. Exceptions and exemptions are not the fence alone. Exceptions and exemptions are not the rescan. Exceptions and exemptions are not the monthly report. Exceptions and exemptions must be renewed or closed. Exceptions and exemptions follow the tolerance boundary. Exceptions and exemptions state residual risk.',
        'r2': 'Exceptions and exemptions are a scaffold permit posted on the fence, naming who allowed the scaffold and the day it must come down. Exceptions and exemptions of a formal waiver are the board minute that says this scaffold design will never match the usual rule. Exceptions and exemptions without a date are a shrug in a frame. Exceptions and exemptions are not the new brickwork, not the rope that keeps pedestrians off the walk, not a second inspection photo, and not the newsletter. Exceptions and exemptions of an expired permit go back to the work list the next morning. Exceptions and exemptions proof is a permit a stranger can read, with a living name and a real end date.',
        'r4': 'Exceptions and exemptions: require owner, reason, residual risk, and a date. Exceptions and exemptions: refuse undated backlog comments. Exceptions and exemptions: match the bar to exposure and tolerance. Exceptions and exemptions: attach the compensating control when one exists. Exceptions and exemptions: surface expired records automatically. Exceptions and exemptions: renew in writing or return the item to remediation. Exceptions and exemptions: keep a person, not only a mailbox. Exceptions and exemptions: separate the decision from the patch and from the rescan.',
        'exam': 'Exceptions and exemptions items are recorded, dated acceptances, never silence. Exceptions and exemptions differ as a time-boxed exception versus a formal waiver. Exceptions and exemptions are not patch remediation, compensating controls, rescanning, or vulnerability reporting. Exceptions and exemptions past their date without renewal are findings of their own. Exceptions and exemptions must fit the stated risk tolerance.',
    },
    'Rescanning and verification': {
        'body': 'Rescanning and verification show, with a repeated check, that the old hit has cleared or that the fence still blocks the unwanted path. Rescanning and verification after a patch should use the same check or an equivalent one and show the previous hit absent. Rescanning and verification must beware a banner that never changes, which can keep a fixed host looking vulnerable or a vulnerable host looking fixed. Rescanning and verification of a compensating control is a path test, not a claim that the underlying flaw number disappeared.\n\nRescanning and verification evidence is the before-and-after result, the date, the tool, and the ticket update. Rescanning and verification is not the act of installing the patch. Rescanning and verification is not the fence design and not the exception signature. Rescanning and verification is not the monthly story told to leadership, though that story should cite it.\n\nRescanning and verification timing should wait until the change has actually propagated, or a hurried scan certifies the old version. Rescanning and verification of a sample of hosts is weak when the change was partial; coverage should match the intended ring. Rescanning and verification failures reopen the change instead of being talked away. Rescanning and verification credentials and scopes must match the original detection path, or a different viewpoint pretends the flaw left.\n\nRescanning and verification can be a version read, a specific request replay, or a scanner plugin, chosen because it actually detects the issue. Rescanning and verification is effective when a ticket cannot close on the patch task alone and the attached result shows the check is clear or the fence test passed. Rescanning and verification methods should be chosen because they detect the original condition, and a screenshot of a login page is rarely that method. Rescanning and verification of a library bump checks the running process or the deployed image digest, not the source repository alone.\n\nRescanning and verification batches can group hosts that shared a change window, and a failure in the batch should name the host. Rescanning and verification of a firewall fence uses a connection attempt from a forbidden source and from an allowed source. Rescanning and verification delays caused by scanner schedules should not leave a ticket closed in anticipation. Rescanning and verification notes should say whether the check was credentialed if the original finding required credentials to see.\n\nRescanning and verification of a partial rollout must not sample only the canary and then close the entire fleet. Rescanning and verification tools can differ from the original scanner if the new tool is shown to detect the issue on a still-vulnerable test host. Rescanning and verification failures are returned to the change owner with the evidence attached. Rescanning and verification records stay with the ticket for the audit retention period.',
        'r0': 'Rescanning and verification close the loop with a repeated check or a control test. Rescanning and verification compare before and after, using a method that can really see the flaw. Rescanning and verification distrust unchanged banners. Rescanning and verification of a fence tests the path and does not pretend the flaw number vanished. Rescanning and verification waits until the rollout has propagated. Rescanning and verification is not the install, the exception, the fence design, or the executive summary. Rescanning and verification proof is an attached result that matches the scope of the change. Rescanning and verification of a library fix should read the running image, not only the source tree. Rescanning and verification must not close a fleet from a canary sample. Rescanning and verification can use a second tool only after that tool catches a still-vulnerable test host.',
        'r1': 'Rescanning and verification prove the outcome. Rescanning and verification repeat a check that can see the flaw. Rescanning and verification compare before and after. Rescanning and verification distrust stale banners. Rescanning and verification test fences with a path check. Rescanning and verification wait until rollout finishes. Rescanning and verification match the original scope. Rescanning and verification reopen failed changes. Rescanning and verification are not the install itself. Rescanning and verification are not the waiver. Rescanning and verification are not the fence design alone. Rescanning and verification are not the leadership summary.',
        'r2': 'Rescanning and verification are the second loaf from the same recipe after the baker changes the yeast. Rescanning and verification taste that loaf rather than trusting the sack label, which still shows the old brand. Rescanning and verification of a rope around a wet floor walk the blocked door instead of claiming the floor is dry. Rescanning and verification happen after the new loaf is actually on the rack, not while the old loaf is still in the oven. Rescanning and verification are not the yeast swap, not the rope, not the permit, and not the note on the cafe board. Rescanning and verification proof is a written taste of the new loaf, or a written walk of the blocked door.',
        'r4': 'Rescanning and verification: rerun a check that can detect the issue. Rescanning and verification: attach before-and-after results. Rescanning and verification: wait for the rollout to finish. Rescanning and verification: cover the same scope as the change. Rescanning and verification: test fences with a denied path. Rescanning and verification: do not trust a banner that never changes. Rescanning and verification: reopen the change if the hit remains. Rescanning and verification: refuse to close on hope.',
        'exam': 'Rescanning and verification items demand evidence that the finding left or that the fence holds. Rescanning and verification are not patch remediation, compensating-control design, exceptions, or vulnerability reporting. Rescanning and verification that uses a different, blind viewpoint can fake success. Rescanning and verification of an unchanged banner needs a better oracle. Rescanning and verification belongs in the ticket before closure.',
    },
    'Vulnerability reporting': {
        'body': 'Vulnerability reporting explains to each audience which issues remain, which fixes landed, which accepts are still in force, and which dates have slipped, using words that audience can act on. Vulnerability reporting to leadership is a short list of open high items with owners and dates, not a raw scan dump. Vulnerability reporting to system owners is specific enough to act, with the finding, the asset, and the expected decision. Vulnerability reporting cadence should be steady so a quiet month is visible rather than skipped.\n\nVulnerability reporting includes exceptions and their end dates, or accepted risk hides. Vulnerability reporting includes verification status, or fixes are claimed without evidence. Vulnerability reporting is not the patch, the fence, the waiver text, or the rescan technique; it is how those facts are communicated. Vulnerability reporting metrics can show aging of the top tier and coverage of the scan, and they should not celebrate a drop that came from muting checks.\n\nVulnerability reporting audiences differ, and a regulator packet is not the same note as an internal stand-up. Vulnerability reporting must protect sensitive exploit detail while still being honest about impact. Vulnerability reporting evidence is the dated note, the audience, and the decisions it asked for. Vulnerability reporting after an incident should match what the incident review later finds, or trust collapses.\n\nVulnerability reporting ownership is a person who can explain the numbers without hiding late items. Vulnerability reporting is effective when a reader can name the next action, the owner, and the date without opening the scanner. Vulnerability reporting calendars should survive vacations, with a deputy author named. Vulnerability reporting of a bad month should still go out, because silence reads as concealment.\n\nVulnerability reporting charts can show age bands for the top tier and a separate line for scan coverage. Vulnerability reporting to a board should avoid exploit detail and should include decisions requested, such as an acceptance above the usual line. Vulnerability reporting to system owners can be a portal view filtered to their assets, refreshed more often than the board note. Vulnerability reporting definitions of fixed, accepted, and open should be written once and reused so two months can be compared.\n\nVulnerability reporting of muted checks should appear as a coverage warning, not as an improvement in the fix rate. Vulnerability reporting after a major incident should align with the incident timeline so the two stories do not contradict. Vulnerability reporting drafts should be reviewed by someone who can spot a missing late item. Vulnerability reporting archives let an auditor see what leadership was told in a given month.\n\nVulnerability reporting of exceptions includes the end date in the same table as the open fixes. Vulnerability reporting tone should be factual, with owners and dates, rather than a pile of alarming adjectives.',
        'r0': 'Vulnerability reporting translates the program into audience-specific updates: open items, fixes, acceptances, and lateness. Vulnerability reporting for executives stays short and names owners and dates. Vulnerability reporting for operators stays actionable. Vulnerability reporting includes exception end dates and whether verification happened. Vulnerability reporting does not treat a muted scanner as progress. Vulnerability reporting is the communication, not the patch, the fence, the waiver, or the rescan method. Vulnerability reporting proof is a dated note that forces a decision a reader can point at. Vulnerability reporting should still go out in a bad month. Vulnerability reporting definitions of open, fixed, and accepted must stay stable across months. Vulnerability reporting of muted checks belongs in a coverage warning. Vulnerability reporting archives show what leadership was told.',
        'r1': 'Vulnerability reporting informs the right audience. Vulnerability reporting lists open items, fixes, and accepts. Vulnerability reporting includes owners and dates. Vulnerability reporting stays short for leadership. Vulnerability reporting stays actionable for operators. Vulnerability reporting includes exception end dates. Vulnerability reporting includes verification status. Vulnerability reporting does not dump raw scan noise upward. Vulnerability reporting does not treat muted checks as success. Vulnerability reporting is not the patch itself. Vulnerability reporting is not the fence itself. Vulnerability reporting is not the waiver itself.',
        'r2': 'Vulnerability reporting is the lighthouse log read to the harbor master at a set hour. Vulnerability reporting says which lamps failed, who is climbing the tower, and which lamp the council agreed to leave dark until spring. Vulnerability reporting does not hand the master every tide table ever printed. Vulnerability reporting is not the climb, not the rope at the base, not the council permit, and not a second look through the lens. Vulnerability reporting of a dark lamp hidden by silence is a failed reading. Vulnerability reporting proof is a master who can point to the next climb and the date without visiting the lens room.',
        'r4': 'Vulnerability reporting: send a steady cadence. Vulnerability reporting: list top open items with owners and dates. Vulnerability reporting: include accepts and their end dates. Vulnerability reporting: include what verification showed. Vulnerability reporting: keep executive notes short. Vulnerability reporting: keep owner notes actionable. Vulnerability reporting: do not count muted checks as improvement. Vulnerability reporting: leave exploit recipes out of wide audiences.',
        'exam': 'Vulnerability reporting items are audience-ready status, not the repair itself. Vulnerability reporting is not patch remediation, compensating controls, exceptions, or rescanning, though it cites them. Vulnerability reporting that pastes raw scanner noise at executives fails the purpose. Vulnerability reporting that hides late items or muted rules is misleading. Vulnerability reporting succeeds when the reader knows the next owner and date.',
    },
}
